{"id": 100, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book After Dark. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) After Dark is a novel by Japanese author Haruki Murakami that follows a group of strangers who convene at a Tokyo bar called \"The Last Bar\" and engage in deep conversations about life, love, and the mysteries of the world until dawn. \n(B) The Rebel, written by Albert Camus, is a philosophical novel that explores the ideas of rebellion, individualism, and the human condition against a backdrop of political oppression and existentialism.\n(C) \"The Rebel\" is a philosophical treatise written by Albert Camus, published in 1951. In this book, Camus explores the concept of rebellion as a fundamental human condition, rejecting traditional forms of rebellion that seek to establish new systems of power. Instead, he advocates for an individual rebellion against oppressive structures, emphasizing the absurdity of human existence and the importance of personal freedom and authenticity. Camus argues that rebellion is not only a political act but also a moral stance, asserting the dignity of the human spirit and the necessity to resist conformity and injustice. Through his examination of historical rebels and fictional characters, Camus reflects on the human condition, the search for meaning, and the ultimate rebellion against death itself.\n(D) After Dark is a novel by Japanese author Haruki Murakami, first published in 1994. The story takes place over the course of a single night in Tokyo, where a group of strangers, including a bar hostess, a writer, a student, and an introverted chef, find themselves connected through a series of unexpected encounters. Set against the backdrop of a mysterious, almost supernatural silence that falls over the city, the characters explore themes of loneliness, human connection, and the search for meaning in their lives. Through their conversations and personal reflections, Murakami weaves a tale that delves into the depths of the human psyche, creating a haunting and thought-provoking narrative that transcends the boundaries of reality."}]}
{"id": 101, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Your Republic Is Calling You. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Your Republic Is Calling You\" is a book that explores the concept of civic duty and engagement in modern society, encouraging readers to actively participate in shaping their communities and country. \n(B) The Cairo Trilogy is a novel series by Naguib Mahfouz that follows the lives of three generations of an Egyptian family in Cairo, exploring themes of tradition, modernity, and personal identity against the backdrop of political and social change in the early 20th century.\n(C) The Cairo Trilogy is a series of three novels by Egyptian author Naguib Mahfouz, consisting of \"Palace Walk,\" \"Children of the Street,\" and \"The Sugar Cane Field.\" Set in Cairo during the early 20th century, the epic family saga follows the lives of the Egyptian middle-class Mubarak family, particularly the patriarch, Al-Sayyid Ahmad, his wife Amina, and their children. The trilogy explores themes of tradition versus modernity, generational conflicts, love, betrayal, and the societal changes sweeping through Egypt at the time, against the backdrop of historical events such as the decline of the Ottoman Empire, the rise of nationalism, and the impact of British colonization. Through its richly drawn characters and intricate narrative, the Cairo Trilogy provides a profound insight into Egyptian culture, religion, and the human condition.\n(D) \"Your Republic Is Calling You\" is a book by Korean-American author Jin Yong (pen name for Louis Cha), which tells the story of a group of young Chinese patriots during the early 20th century, who risk their lives to fight for the country's independence and modernization. Set against the backdrop of historical events like the辛亥革命 (Xinhai Revolution) and the rise of抗日战争 (War of Resistance Against Japan), the novel combines elements of action, romance, and political intrigue to illustrate the enduring spirit of sacrifice and national unity among the Chinese people. Through its compelling characters and vivid storytelling, the book aims to inspire readers to actively contribute to their nation's progress."}]}
{"id": 102, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Haroun and the Sea of Stories. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Haroun and the Sea of Stories is a novel by Salman Rushdie, narrating the journey of a young boy named Haroun, whose father, the famous storyteller Shamsur Rahman, is struck dumb by an evil force called the \"Age of Silence.\" Haroun embarks on a magical adventure to rescue his father's lost gift of storytelling by journeying through the Sea of Stories, encountering mythical creatures, and battling the monstrous Shadow, who seeks to destroy creativity. Along the way, Haroun learns the power of words, the importance of imagination, and the resilience of the human spirit in the face of oppression. Through his quest, he restores the flow of stories, saving not only his father but also reviving the world's joy and wonder. \n(B) \"Consciousness Explained\" is a book by philosopher Daniel Dennett, in which he offers a comprehensive and highly influential account of the nature of consciousness. Dennett argues that consciousness is an emergent property of complex information-processing systems, rather than a mysterious, non-physical phenomenon. He rejects dualistic views that separate mind and body, and instead proposes a materialist explanation, emphasizing the role of integration, attention, and the subjective sense of narrative flow in our conscious experience. Dennett draws on insights from neuroscience, cognitive science, and artificial intelligence to construct a detailed and dynamic model of how consciousness arises from the interactions within the brain.\n(C) Haroun and the Sea of Stories is a novel by Salman Rushdie that follows a young boy named Haroun who embarks on a magical journey to save the stories that have been stolen from his father, the famous storyteller.\n(D) \"Consciousness Explained\" by Daniel Dennett is a philosophical exploration that presents a comprehensive and integrative account of consciousness, drawing on insights from cognitive science, neuroscience, and philosophy to explain the nature and function of conscious experience."}]}
{"id": 103, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Mine Boy. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Structure of Scientific Revolutions\" by Thomas S. Kuhn is a groundbreaking work in the philosophy of science, published in 1962. Kuhn argues that the progression of science is not linear, but rather occurs in cycles of normal science, where researchers work within a dominant paradigm, followed by periods of crisis that lead to revolutionary changes, ultimately giving rise to a new paradigm. He emphasizes that scientific paradigms shape how scientists perceive, interpret, and prioritize evidence, making it difficult for them to recognize inconsistencies within the prevailing framework. Kuhn's book challenges the notion of progress as a steady accumulation of knowledge, suggesting that scientific progress is more episodic and subject to societal and cognitive factors. \n(B) \"Mine Boy\" is a semi-autobiographical novel written by South African author Solomon T. Plaatje, first published in 1923. The novel provides a vivid and insightful account of the harsh conditions faced by black miners in the gold mines of South Africa during the early 20th century. Through the eyes of the protagonist, a young African boy named Silas, the story narrates his journey from rural life to working in the mines, exposing the exploitation, racial discrimination, and poverty endured by black workers. Plaatje skillfully intertwines the personal struggles of Silas with the broader political and social context of the time, shedding light on the oppressive apartheid system and the early labor movements that fought against it. \"Mine Boy\" serves as both a powerful social commentary and a testament to the resilience of the human spirit.\n(C) \"The Structure of Scientific Revolutions\" by Thomas Kuhn argues that scientific progress is not a smooth linear process, but rather occurs through periodic revolutions where old paradigms are replaced by new ones when anomalies accumulate and a critical mass of alternative ideas emerges.\n(D) \"Mine Boy\" by Solomon Tlali is a semi-autobiographical novel that portrays the harsh realities of life for black miners in South Africa during the apartheid era, narrated through the eyes of a young boy growing up in the mining industry."}]}
{"id": 104, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Oryx and Crake. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Oryx and Crake is a dystopian novel by Margaret Atwood that follows the story of a man named Snowman, living in a world ravaged by genetic engineering and environmental collapse, as he recounts his past and the creation of two bioengineered entities, Oryx and Crake. \n(B) \"The Mote in God's Eye\" is a science fiction novel co-written by Larry Niven and Jerry Pournelle, published in 1974. It tells the story of humanity's first contact with an alien civilization, the Moties, who inhabit a star system with a gas giant planet. The Moties are a highly intelligent, but physically limited species, with a complex societal structure. When humans establish communication, they discover that the Moties are technologically inferior but possess unique abilities and strategies. The two species form an uneasy alliance as they face a common threat from a powerful, expansionist alien race called the Bussards. The novel explores themes of cultural differences, diplomacy, and the challenges of interstellar warfare, as the humans and Moties work together to protect their worlds and understand the complexities of the galaxy.\n(C) \"The Mote in God's Eye\" is a science fiction novel by Larry Niven and Jerry Pournelle that depicts a tense encounter between humans and an alien civilization, the Moties, in a distant star system, exploring themes of cultural clash, diplomacy, and survival.\n(D) Oryx and Crake is a dystopian novel by Margaret Atwood, set in a future world ravaged by genetic engineering and environmental collapse. The story is told through the eyes of Snowman, one of the few surviving humans, as he recounts the events that led to the collapse of civilization. The novel follows the complex relationship between two bioengineers, Crake and Jimmy (who becomes Snowman), and their experiments with genetic manipulation, creating exotic species and altering human beings. As their creations spiral out of control, leading to global pandemics and social unrest, the consequences of their actions ultimately result in the downfall of humanity. Atwood explores themes of scientific ethics, corporate power, and the potential dangers of unchecked technological advancement."}]}
{"id": 105, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Three Day Road. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Three Day Road is a novel by Joseph Boyden that follows the lives of two Cree soldiers and their journey home after fighting in World War I, exploring themes of friendship, trauma, and the impact of colonization on Indigenous peoples. \n(B) \"Things I've Been Silent About\" is a collection of essays by Alice Walker in which she explores personal experiences and societal issues, delving into topics such as race, gender, sexuality, and spirituality with profound honesty and vulnerability.\n(C) \"Things I've Been Silent About\" is a collection of essays by acclaimed author and activist Alice Walker, in which she explores various aspects of her personal life and the social issues that have shaped her worldview. Through intimate narratives, Walker delves into her childhood experiences, racial and gender inequalities, spirituality, activism, and the complexities of relationships. The book serves as a candid reflection on her silence and the courage it took to speak up about her own struggles and the injustices she has witnessed, offering a powerful testament to resilience and the importance of giving voice to one's experiences.\n(D) Three Day Road by Joseph Boyden is a novel that explores the complex relationship between two Cree brothers, Xavier and Roland, who serve as snipers for the Canadian Army during World War I. Set against the backdrop of the brutal conflict, the story delves into their intertwined lives, their shared heritage, and the impact of war on their identity. As they fight on the front lines, the brothers navigate the complexities of loyalty, brotherhood, and the devastating consequences of violence, while also grappling with the cultural clash between their traditional Indigenous roots and the modern world. Haunted by personal demons and the memories of their past, their journey becomes a quest for redemption and a search for meaning in a world torn apart by war."}]}
{"id": 106, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Lives of Girls and Women. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Just Mercy\" is a book by Bryan Stevenson that chronicles his experiences as a lawyer defending marginalized individuals, particularly those wrongfully convicted of crimes, and shines a light on systemic injustice in the American criminal justice system. \n(B) \"Lives of Girls and Women\" is a novel by Canadian author Alice Munro, which tells the story of the coming-of-age of a young woman named Delia in a small town in Ontario. Through a series of interconnected stories spanning several years, Munro explores Delia's relationships with her family, friends, and romantic interests, as well as her gradual development of self-awareness and independence. The novel delves into themes of identity, female sexuality, rural life, and the complex dynamics of interpersonal connections, showcasing Munro's masterful storytelling and nuanced characterizations.\n(C) \"Lives of Girls and Women\" by Alice Munro is a novel that explores the coming-of-age experiences and complex relationships of a young woman in a small Canadian town during the mid-20th century.\n(D) \"Just Mercy\" is a powerful and inspiring memoir by Bryan Stevenson, a renowned lawyer and social justice advocate. The book chronicles his experiences as the founder of the Equal Justice Initiative (EJI), a nonprofit organization dedicated to fighting for the rights of marginalized individuals, particularly those on death row who have been wrongfully convicted or unfairly sentenced. Through gripping narratives, Stevenson sheds light on the systemic racism and injustice present in the American criminal justice system, sharing the stories of his clients, their families, and the broader struggle for human rights. His journey highlights the importance of compassion, perseverance, and the need for profound societal change to ensure equality and justice for all."}]}
{"id": 107, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Luminaries. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The Luminaries is an award-winning novel by Eleanor Catton, set in 1860s New Zealand during the gold rush. It follows a complex web of interconnected characters, including a mysterious woman named Anna芙蓉, a luckless opium dealer, a scheming lawyer, and a resourceful detective, as they navigate love, greed, and intrigue in the isolated town of Hokitika. Blending elements of detective fiction, historical narrative, and astrological symbolism, the novel explores themes of fate, fortune, and the human drive for redemption, while its intricate plot unfolds like a celestial puzzle, culminating in a dramatic resolution. \n(B) Big Little Lies is a TV series that explores the lives of wealthy and seemingly perfect mothers in a small coastal town, whose lives become intertwined and secrets are revealed through a murder investigation.\n(C) Big Little Lies is a gripping and dramatic television series, based on the novel by Liane Moriarty, that explores the lives of three seemingly perfect mothers in the opulent town of Monterey, California. As their lives intertwine, secrets, lies, and betrayals come to surface, leading to a murder mystery that unravels the facade of their picturesque lives. The show delves into themes of friendship, marriage, parenthood, and the dark underbelly of suburban life, all while delivering powerful performances and intense plot twists.\n(D) The Luminaries is a historical fiction novel by Eleanor Catton that follows a group of diverse characters in 1860s New Zealand as they navigate love, greed, and mystery in their pursuit of wealth during the gold rush."}]}
{"id": 108, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Picnic at Hanging Rock. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Last Man\" is a novel by Mary Shelley, published in 1826. It presents a post-apocalyptic world where a mysterious plague has wiped out nearly all of humanity, leaving only a few scattered survivors. The protagonist, Lionel Verney, grapples with the孤独 and existential questions that arise in a world without society. As he navigates the desolate landscape, encountering other survivors and reflecting on the past, Verney explores themes of human nature, morality, and the potential for redemption in a world on the brink of extinction. Through Verney's experiences, Shelley critiques societal norms and imagines a new order emerging from the ashes of civilization. \n(B) \"Picnic at Hanging Rock,\" a novel by Joan Lindsay, tells the story of a mysterious disappearance during a school outing to the enigmatic Hanging Rock in rural Australia in 1900. Four girls and their teacher vanish without a trace, leaving the community baffled and haunted by the event. The narrative weaves together elements of mystery, romance, and the supernatural, as the search for the missing individuals uncovers the town's secrets and the eerie powers of the ancient rock formation. The novel explores themes of the conflict between civilization and the untamed wilderness, the mysteries of the Australian landscape, and the constraints of Victorian society.\n(C) \"The Last Man\" is a novel by Mary Shelley that envisions a post-apocalyptic world where a pandemic has wiped out most of humanity, and the last surviving man grapples with loneliness, morality, and the meaning of existence.\n(D) \"Picnic at Hanging Rock\" is a novel by Joan Lindsay that tells the mysterious story of three girls and a teacher who vanish during a school outing to a remote rock formation in Australia, exploring themes of mystery, the allure of the unknown, and the clash between civilization and the untamed wilderness."}]}
{"id": 109, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Brighton Rock. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The Black Obelisk is a novel by Nigerian author Chinua Achebe, published in 1964. It is a historical fiction that tells the story of Ezra, a young Igbo man who becomes the chief sculptor in the ancient kingdom of Nri. The novel explores themes of power, tradition, and cultural identity as Ezra rises to prominence, creates a legendary black obelisk statue, and navigates the complex political landscape of the kingdom. Through Ezra's experiences, Achebe delves into the rich cultural heritage of pre-colonial Nigeria, shedding light on the intricate social structures, spiritual beliefs, and artistic expressions of the time. The Black Obelisk is a powerful work that combines historical accuracy with imaginative storytelling, offering readers a captivating glimpse into Africa's past. \n(B) \"The Black Obelisk\" is a novel by Nigerian author Chinua Achebe that explores themes of colonialism, cultural identity, and power dynamics through the story of an ancient artifact's impact on a fictional African kingdom.\n(C) Brighton Rock is a novel by Graham Greene, first published in 1938. The story is set in the seaside town of Brighton, England, and revolves around the life of a young, ruthless gangster named Pinkie Brown. Pinkie, a member of a local criminal gang, becomes embroiled in a murder plot after he kills a rival gang member to protect his own position. As Pinkie tries to evade detection by the police and a persistent journalist, he becomes involved with a naive young waitress named Rose Armitage, whom he manipulates into helping him. The novel explores themes of morality, redemption, and the consequences of violence, as Pinkie's actions have a profound impact on the lives of those around him, leading to a dramatic and haunting conclusion.\n(D) Brighton Rock is a crime novel by Graham Greene that follows a young gangster's descent into violence and moral corruption in the seaside town of Brighton, England."}]}
{"id": 110, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book A Burnt-Out Case. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The Apology is a dialogue by Plato in which Socrates defends his philosophical life and ideas against charges of impiety and corruption, leading to his conviction and sentence of death. \n(B) The Apology is a dialogue by the ancient Greek philosopher Plato, believed to be based on the actual defense speech of Socrates at his trial in Athens in 399 BCE. In the dialogue, Socrates faces charges of corrupting the youth and impiety, stemming from his unorthodox teaching methods and questioning of traditional beliefs. Socrates defends his actions, asserting that his pursuit of wisdom and knowledge is a noble endeavor and that he has always acted in the best interest of Athens. He refuses to compromise his philosophical principles or pay lip service to the gods to secure his freedom, maintaining his belief in his divine mission. The Apology ultimately portrays Socrates as a man of integrity, who chooses death over compromising his commitment to truth and philosophical inquiry.\n(C) A Burnt-Out Case is a novel by Graham Greene, published in 1961, that follows the spiritual and psychological journey of a former colonial administrator, Querry, who retreats to a remote African mission after becoming disillusioned with his past life and haunted by a sense of guilt. Seeking redemption and escape from his burnt-out existence, Querry takes on the role of a priest in a leper colony. As he grapples with his own faith, morality, and the complexities of colonial legacy, he becomes entangled in a微妙 relationship with a beautiful expatriate, Sarah, and becomes involved in the微妙 political tensions brewing in the region. The novel explores themes of redemption, love, morality, and the lingering effects of colonialism on both the colonizer and the colonized.\n(D) A Burnt-Out Case is a novel by Graham Greene that follows a disillusioned former colonial administrator in Africa as he seeks redemption and escape from his past by hiding in a remote mission station, where his encounter with a young idealist leads to a crisis of faith and identity."}]}
{"id": 111, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Franchise Affair. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Uprooted is a novel by Naomi Novik that combines elements of fairy tale and fantasy, following a young woman named Agnieszka who discovers her magical abilities and must navigate a dangerous world of politics, magic, and a malevolent sorcerer known as the Dragon. \n(B) \"The Franchise Affair\" is a novel by British author Anthony Berkeley Cox, first published in 1948. The story revolves around a middle-aged couple, Mr. and Mrs. Petherick, who claim that a young woman, Audrey Long, had posed as their niece and lived with them for a while before disappearing. They allege that Audrey had orchestrated a fraudulent scheme to deceive them. However, Audrey suddenly reappears, denying any wrongdoing and insisting that she was kidnapped by the Pethericks. As the truth becomes increasingly blurred, a lawyer and a journalist become entangled in the case, investigating the conflicting narratives and uncovering surprising secrets that question the reliability of memory and the nature of truth. The novel is a psychological thriller that explores themes of identity, deception, and the complexities of human relationships.\n(C) \"The Franchise Affair\" is a novel by British author Anthony Berkeley Cox, in which a woman's claim of being kidnapped is investigated, leading to a complex web of lies, deceit, and mistaken identity.\n(D) Uprooted is a novel by Naomi Novik that combines elements of fantasy and fairy tale. It tells the story of Agnieszka, a young woman living in a Polish-inspired village on the edge of the magical and dangerous Wood. When the powerful and enigmatic Dragon, the region's protective wizard, chooses Agnieszka's best friend for his annual apprenticeship, Agnieszka is unexpectedly selected instead. As she embarks on a life unlike any she could have imagined, Agnieszka discovers her own hidden talents, learns to wield magic, and becomes entangled in a battle to save her village and the Wood from an ancient evil. Through her journey, she learns the true meaning of friendship, love, and the transformative power of courage."}]}
{"id": 112, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Three Stigmata of Palmer Eldritch. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Labyrinth of the Spirits\" by Carlos Ruiz Zafón is a novel that intertwines the lives of several characters through generations, exploring love, loss, and the enduring power of literature against the backdrop of post-Civil War Spain. \n(B) \"The Three Stigmata of Palmer Eldritch\" is a science fiction novel by Philip K. Dick, published in 1965. The story takes place in a dystopian future where humanity has colonized the Solar System, but is plagued by societal decay and the widespread use of a powerful hallucinogenic drug called Can-D. The novel follows an advertising executive, Bob Arctor, who becomes embroiled in a battle of perceptions between the enigmatic Palmer Eldritch, a wealthy and influential entrepreneur who has introduced a rival drug, and an underground resistance fighting against the drug's mind-altering effects. As Arctor's reality blurs and his sense of self disintegrates, the novel explores themes of identity, perception, and the nature of reality in a world where truth is subjective and easily manipulated.\n(C) \"The Three Stigmata of Palmer Eldritch\" by Philip K. Dick is a science fiction novel that explores the consequences of advanced technology and drug-induced realities in a dystopian future.\n(D) \"The Labyrinth of the Spirits\" by Carlos Ruiz Zafón is a captivating novel that intertwines history, love, and mystery. Set in post-Civil War Spain, it follows the story of young writer徐ia Alazán, who becomes entangled in the lives of the Cemetery of Forgotten Books' last守护者, Sempere, and the enigmatic writer, Beatriz Montesinos. Unraveling a web of secrets,徐ia discovers a dark history involving a dangerous political regime, lost manuscripts, and a generations-old family curse. Through her journey, she learns about the power of literature and the resilience of the human spirit in the face of adversity. The novel is a tribute to the enduring power of books and the importance of preserving memory against the forces of forgetting."}]}
{"id": 113, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book More Than Human. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) They Burn the Thistles is a novel by Scottish author A.L. Kennedy that explores the lives of a group of people in a small Scottish town, delving into themes of love, loss, and the complexities of human relationships. \n(B) \"More Than Human\" is a book by Brian David Johnson that explores the future of human enhancement and the potential merging of humans with technology. It delves into the ethical, social, and philosophical implications of advancements in fields such as artificial intelligence, biotechnology, and robotics, envisioning a world where humans can augment their cognitive, physical, and emotional capabilities. Johnson invites readers to consider the possibilities and challenges that lie ahead as we blur the lines between what it means to be human and machine, ultimately questioning the very essence of our humanity.\n(C) \"More Than Human\" is a book that explores the intersection of technology, biology, and humanity, envisioning a future where humans enhance themselves and merge with machines to become post-human.\n(D) They Burn the Thistles is a novel by Scottish author A.L. Kennedy that explores the life of a young woman named Moira in 1950s rural Scotland. The story delves into Moira's challenging upbringing in a poverty-stricken family, her complex relationships, and the societal constraints she faces. Through Moira's resilient spirit and her connection with nature, the novel examines themes of resilience, love, loss, and the power of memory. Kennedy's vivid and poetic writing captures the harsh realities of Moira's world while also revealing moments of tenderness and hope amidst the adversity."}]}
{"id": 114, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Iron Council. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Golovlyov Family\" is a novel by Russian author Ivan Turgenev that portrays the decline of an aristocratic family in the mid-19th century, reflecting societal changes and moral decay in pre-revolutionary Russia. \n(B) \"The Golovlyov Family\" is a novel by Russian author Ivan Turgenev, first published in 1862. It tells the story of the decline of the aristocratic Golovlyov family over three generations, set against the backdrop of 19th-century Russian society. The narrative revolves around the lives of the eccentric and dissolute family members, their struggles, conflicts, and the consequences of their hedonistic lifestyles. Through the characters' experiences, Turgenev explores themes of moral decay, the collapse of the traditional aristocracy, and the search for meaning in a changing world. The novel is known for its vivid portrayal of Russian society, psychological depth, and Turgenev's signature style of combining humor with social critique.\n(C) Iron Council is a science fiction novel by China Miéville, set in a richly imagined alternate world where steampunk technology and magical elements coexist. The story follows a young man named Ferro, who lives in a sprawling, constantly growing city called New Crobuzon. Ferro becomes involved with a group of rebels known as the \"Iron Council,\" who are fighting against the oppressive ruling class and the destructive forces of industrialization. As Ferro's journey takes him through diverse landscapes and encounters with fantastical creatures, the novel explores themes of revolution, class struggle, and the complex relationship between humanity and the environment. Miéville's vivid prose and intricate world-building create a compelling narrative that challenges conventional notions of power and explores the resilience of the human spirit in the face of adversity.\n(D) Iron Council is a post-apocalyptic fantasy novel by China Miéville, set in a world of steampunk and magical realism, where a group of rebels fight against an oppressive ruling class and their powerful iron-made machines."}]}
{"id": 115, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Earthsea Quartet. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Kokoro is a novel by Japanese author Natsume Soseki that explores the themes of friendship, love, and the human heart through the story of three young students in Tokyo. \n(B) The Earthsea Quartet is a series of four fantasy novels written by Ursula K. Le Guin, set in a archipelagic world called Earthsea. The story follows the young wizard Ged, whose reckless use of magic unintendedly releases a dangerous shadow creature. On a journey of redemption and self-discovery, Ged matures through trials, encounters mythical creatures, and learns the balance between power and wisdom. The series explores themes of balance, harmony, the consequences of action, and the intricate relationship between humanity and the natural world. Through Ged's adventures, Le Guin creates a richly imagined, immersive world filled with magic, mythology, and profound philosophical insights.\n(C) The Earthsea Quartet is a series of fantasy novels by Ursula K. Le Guin, following the journey of a young wizard named Ged as he navigates a world of magic, danger, and self-discovery.\n(D) Kokoro is a novel by Japanese author Natsume Soseki, first published in 1914. The story revolves around the life of a young man named Kazuma, who moves to Tokyo to pursue his studies and dreams. Through his encounters with various characters, particularly his close friend, Kamiya, and the enigmatic beauty, Chie, Kazuma experiences the complexities of human relationships, love, and the fleeting nature of life. Kokoro delves into themes of loneliness, the conflict between tradition and modernity, and the search for meaning and connection in a rapidly changing society, offering a poignant portrayal of the human condition."}]}
{"id": 116, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Gormenghast. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Gormenghast is a series of three novels by Mervyn Peake, comprising \"Gormenghast Castle,\" \"Titus Groan,\" and \"Titus Alone.\" The books are set in a sprawling, decaying medieval-style castle and follow the life of Titus Groan, the 77th Earl of Gormenghast. The narrative delves into the intricate rituals, power struggles, and dark secrets within the castle's walls, as well as the conflict between tradition and individuality. The series is known for its rich, atmospheric prose, complex characters, and a surreal, almost dreamlike quality that blurs the lines between reality and fantasy. \n(B) Gormenghast is a gothic novel by Mervyn Peake, following the lives of the inhabitants of the vast and decaying castle of Gormenghast, as a young man named Steerpike rises through the ranks and threatens the stability of the oppressive regime.\n(C) \"The Third Man\" is a 1949 film noir directed by Carol Reed, starring Orson Welles and Joseph Cotten. It tells the story of Holly Martins, an American writer who travels to post-war Vienna to take up a job offered by his old friend Harry Lime, only to find out that Lime has recently died in a suspicious accident. As Holly investigates Lime's death, he becomes increasingly drawn into a web of intrigue, betrayal, and the corrupt atmosphere of the divided city. With its iconic zither score, haunting visuals, and a memorable performance by Welles as the enigmatic Lime, \"The Third Man\" is a classic tale of morality, friendship, and the darkness lurking beneath the surface of a war-torn society.\n(D) \"The Third Man\" is a 1949 film noir that follows an American writer in post-war Vienna who becomes entangled in a web of mystery and deception surrounding his enigmatic friend's suspicious death."}]}
{"id": 117, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Room. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Room is a heart-wrenching novel by Emma Donoghue that tells the story of Jack, a five-year-old boy who has lived his entire life inside a single room with his mother, who was kidnapped and held captive by a man known as \"Old Nick.\" To Jack, Room is the entire world, and he is unaware of the existence of anything beyond its four walls. His mother, Ma, has shielded him from the残酷 truth and created a loving, nurturing environment within their confined space. As the plot unfolds, the pair's resilience and determination to escape Room and confront the outside world, known to Jack as \"Outside,\" form the basis of their harrowing journey towards freedom and a new life. \n(B) Room is a heart-wrenching novel about a young mother and her son who are held captive in a single room and their miraculous escape to freedom.\n(C) The Dispossessed is a science fiction novel by Ursula K. Le Guin, published in 1974. It tells the story of Shevek, a physicist from the anarchist, communal planet of Anarres, who travels to the capitalist, hierarchical world of Urras in search of scientific collaboration. The novel explores themes of social structures, political ideologies, individual freedom, and the consequences of economic systems on personal relationships and societal well-being. Through Shevek's experiences on both planets, Le Guin offers a nuanced critique of capitalism and a utopian vision of anarchism, highlighting the potential for cooperation, empathy, and the importance of balancing individual and collective needs.\n(D) The Dispossessed is a science fiction novel by Ursula K. Le Guin that explores the themes of anarchism, capitalism, and cooperation through the story of a physicist who travels between two planets with contrasting social systems."}]}
{"id": 118, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book King Rat. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) King Rat is a novel by James Clavell set in a Japanese prisoner of war camp during World War II, which follows the lives of British soldiers and their struggle for survival, power, and morality, centering around the character of King Rat. \n(B) \"The Information\" is a book written by James Gleick that explores the history and nature of information, from the dawn of human communication to the digital age. It delves into the concepts of data, knowledge, and meaning, examining how information has shaped society, technology, and our understanding of the world. Gleick discusses key figures such as Claude Shannon, who laid the foundation for modern information theory, and how their ideas have revolutionized fields like cryptography, computing, and telecommunications. The book also touches on the challenges and implications of living in an era where information is abundant, accessible, and constantly evolving.\n(C) \"The Information\" by James Gleick is a book that explores the history, nature, and impact of information, from the dawn of human communication to the digital age.\n(D) King Rat is a novel by James Clavell set in a Japanese prisoner of war camp during World War II. It follows the lives of British and American soldiers, focusing on the complex hierarchy and survival strategies within the camp. The main character, Corporal King, is a charismatic and cunning British soldier who becomes the de facto leader of the prisoners, engaging in black market activities to maintain his power and influence. The novel explores themes of morality, camaraderie, and the lengths people will go to survive in extreme circumstances, while also delving into the moral ambiguities of leadership and the human capacity for both kindness and brutality."}]}
{"id": 119, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Thinking, Fast and Slow. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Goodbye Tsugumi\" is a coming-of-age story that follows three high school friends as they navigate their final summer together, dealing with love, loss, and the bittersweet transition into adulthood. \n(B) \"Thinking, Fast and Slow\" by Daniel Kahneman is a groundbreaking book that delves into the intricacies of human thought processes, dividing them into two systems: System 1, which is fast, intuitive, and emotional; and System 2, slower, more rational, and deliberate. The book explores how these systems interact, influence our decision-making, and lead to common cognitive biases. Kahneman provides insightful examples and anecdotes to illustrate how these biases affect our judgments in various aspects of life, from personal relationships to financial investments, and he offers strategies to improve decision-making by harnessing the power of slow thinking. The book is a compelling blend of psychology, economics, and behavioral science, transforming our understanding of the mind and its limitations.\n(C) \"Thinking, Fast and Slow\" by Daniel Kahneman is a book that explores the dual systems of human thought, highlighting the cognitive biases, heuristics, and emotional influences that shape our decision-making processes.\n(D) \"Goodbye Tsugumi\" is a heartwarming and poignant manga series by Japan's female manga-ka, Natsumi Ando. The story revolves around the lives of three childhood friends, Tsugumi, Koyuki, and Chinatsu, as they navigate the challenges of adolescence, love, and the inevitable passage of time. As the friends grow older, their paths diverge, leading to bittersweet experiences, self-discovery, and the realization that some goodbyes are necessary for personal growth. The series beautifully captures the complexities of friendship, the pain of loss, and the enduring power of memories, all while exploring the themes of change, acceptance, and the fleeting nature of youth."}]}
{"id": 120, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Demon-Haunted World. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Moderato Cantabile is a novel by Italian author Margherita Yourcenar, first published in 1958. The story revolves around the life of a fictional 19th-century French composer, Jules Barret, and his complex relationship with a talented young pianist, Paul Sabatier. Through a series of letters and diary entries, the narrative explores themes of artistic passion, mentorship, and the sacrifices made in the pursuit of love and creativity. Yourcenar masterfully weaves together elements of historical fiction, psychological insight, and musical inspiration, creating a profound and intimate portrayal of the characters' inner worlds and the beauty and pain of artistic expression. \n(B) \"The Demon-Haunted World: Science as a Candle in the Dark\" is a book by Carl Sagan that advocates for critical thinking, skepticism, and the importance of scientific reasoning in a world filled with misinformation, superstition, and pseudoscience. Sagan encourages readers to embrace the rational and evidence-based approach of science to navigate through the \"demons\" of ignorance and deception, and to use critical thinking as a tool to illuminate our understanding of the universe. He emphasizes the need for a scientifically literate society to make informed decisions and to appreciate the beauty and wonder of the natural world, free from the constraints of superstition and blind belief.\n(C) \"The Demon-Haunted World: Science as a Candle in the Dark\" by Carl Sagan is a book that advocates for critical thinking, scientific literacy, and rationality in a world filled with misinformation and supernatural beliefs.\n(D) \"Moderato Cantabile\" is a musical term that describes a moderate tempo with a singing, melodic quality."}]}
{"id": 121, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book At Home. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"At Home\" by Bill Bryson is a humorous and informative exploration of the history and science of everyday objects and spaces within the average home. \n(B) The Graveyard Book by Neil Gaiman is a heartwarming and suspenseful coming-of-age story about a boy named Bod who grows up in a graveyard, protected by its supernatural inhabitants, while pursued by the vengeful ghost of his murdered father's killer.\n(C) The Graveyard Book is a children's fantasy novel by Neil Gaiman, which tells the story of a boy named Bod who is raised by ghosts in a graveyard after his family is murdered. With the help of his ghostly guardians and the mysterious \"Silent Army,\" Bod learns the art of the无声 walk, the dangerous game of hide-and-seek, and the importance of friendship and bravery. As he grows older, Bod must confront the evil that hunting him, the man Jack, while navigating the divide between the living and the dead. Through his adventures, Bod learns the value of life, love, and the connection between the worlds of the living and the graveyard.\n(D) \"At Home\" is a book by Bill Bryson that takes readers on a journey through the history and science of the everyday objects and spaces that make up our homes. Bryson explores the origins, evolution, and significance of various aspects of domestic life, from plumbing and electricity to furniture and cooking utensils. With his characteristic wit and engaging writing style, Bryson delves into the fascinating stories behind these familiar elements, revealing the incredible innovations and historical context that have shaped the way we live. The book offers a rich and often surprising insight into the overlooked aspects of our homes, highlighting their impact on human history and our daily experiences."}]}
{"id": 122, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Radical Chic. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Radical Chic\" is a satirical term coined by Tom Wolfe to describe the fashionable and superficial support of radical political causes by wealthy and privileged individuals, often without a deep understanding or commitment to the underlying issues. \n(B) The Golden Bowl is a novel by Henry James that follows the complex relationships and moral dilemmas of two American sisters and their English suitors, revealing the consequences of concealed truths and societal expectations in their aristocratic world.\n(C) The Golden Bowl is a novel by Henry James, published in 1904. It tells the story of two American sisters, Maggie and Charlotte, who are married to two English aristocrats, Prince Amerigo and Sir Paul Pomfret, respectively. The narrative revolves around the intricate web of relationships, secrets, and betrayals that unfold among the characters. As the plot thickens, it becomes evident that Amerigo is having an affair with Charlotte, while Maggie is unaware of this secret. The golden bowl, a precious family heirloom, symbolizes the delicate and fragile nature of their relationships. The novel explores themes of love, deception, class, and the clash of American and European cultures, ultimately leading to a tragic revelation and the shattering of the golden bowl.\n(D) \"Radical Chic\" is a term coined by satirical writer Tom Wolfe in his 1970 essay, which describes the phenomenon of wealthy, liberal, and often culturally elite individuals embracing radical political causes, particularly those associated with the Black Panther Party, in a superficial and self-indulgent manner. These socialites would host fashionable parties for Panther leaders, financially support the group, and display their progressive credentials, often without a deep understanding or commitment to the underlying issues. Wolfe's term critiques the superficiality of these social trends and the insensitivity towards the genuine struggles faced by the oppressed groups."}]}
{"id": 123, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Autobiography of Malcolm X. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Peter Pan is a beloved children's story about a mischievous boy who refuses to grow up and has magical adventures in Neverland. He rescues Wendy, John, and Michael from their dull lives in London and takes them to his island home, where they join his band of Lost Boys and fight against the villainous Captain Hook. With the help of Tinker Bell and her fairy friends, Peter and the children embark on thrilling escapades, encountering mermaids, Indians, and a ticking crocodile. The tale explores the themes of imagination, childhood innocence, and the bittersweet inevitability of growing up. \n(B) The Autobiography of Malcolm X, written in collaboration with Alex Haley, chronicles the life of Malcolm X, a prominent African American civil rights activist. It details his journey from a troubled youth in Omaha, to his time in prison where he converted to Islam and became a leader in the Nation of Islam,再到 his pilgrimage to Mecca that led to his evolution in beliefs and his departure from the Nation of Islam to form the Organization of Afro-American Unity. The book powerfully recounts Malcolm X's fight for racial equality, his advocacy for black self-determination, and his transformation from a fiery advocate of black nationalism to a more inclusive vision of human rights.\n(C) The Autobiography of Malcolm X is an influential memoir co-written by Malcolm X and Alex Haley, detailing Malcolm's life from his early years as a troubled youth to his transformation into a prominent civil rights activist and leader.\n(D) Peter Pan is a beloved children's story about a boy who never grows up, his adventures in Neverland with pirates, mermaids, and fairies, and his encounters with the villainous Captain Hook."}]}
{"id": 124, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The New Jim Crow. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The New Jim Crow is a book by Michelle Alexander that argues that the criminal justice system in the United States functions as a contemporary form of racial control and oppression, particularly targeting Black Americans. \n(B) Christine is a novel by Stephen King, published in 1983. It follows the story of Christine, a 1958 red and white Ford Fury that is possessed by a malevolent spirit, which brings out the dark desires and aggression in its owners. The car becomes a symbol of evil, as it repairs itself, manipulates its owners, and seeks revenge against those who wrong them. The novel explores themes of obsession, possession, and the power of memory, as the protagonist, Arnie Cunningham, becomes increasingly consumed by Christine and his relationship with the car threatens his friendships, love life, and ultimately his sanity.\n(C) Christine is a novel by Stephen King about a possessed car that seeks revenge against those who wrong its owner.\n(D) The New Jim Crow, written by Michelle Alexander, is a groundbreaking book that examines how the U.S. criminal justice system has perpetuated racial inequality and discrimination, resembling the Jim Crow laws of the past. Alexander argues that mass incarceration, Draconian drug policies, and racial profiling have created a new system of social control, targeting Black Americans and systematically denying them equal rights and opportunities. She asserts that this \"war on drugs\" is a thinly veiled attempt to disfranchise and marginalize a large segment of the African American population, resulting in a modern-day form of racial apartheid. The book calls for a comprehensive reevaluation of American society's understanding of justice and the urgent need for systemic change to dismantle this new Jim Crow."}]}
{"id": 125, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Grit. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Night Watch is a novel by Sergei Lukyanenko that follows a Moscow-based night watchman, Anton Gorodetsky, who discovers a hidden world of supernatural beings and becomes embroiled in a centuries-old conflict between vampires and witches. \n(B) \"Grit\" is a book written by psychologist Angela Duckworth, which explores the concept of grit as a key factor in achieving success. Duckworth defines grit as the combination of passion and perseverance for long-term goals, distinguishing it from talent or intelligence. Through extensive research and real-life examples, she argues that individuals with grit are more likely to overcome obstacles, persist in their efforts, and ultimately achieve their ambitions, regardless of their initial circumstances or abilities. \"Grit\" emphasizes the importance of cultivating a growth mindset, developing resilience, and fostering meaningful pursuits to foster grit and reach one's full potential.\n(C) Night Watch is a novel by Russian author Sergei Lukyanenko, set in a world where vampires and witches exist and are secretly divided into two factions, the Light and the Dark Others. The story follows Anton Gorodetsky, a young man who discovers he is an \"Other\" with supernatural abilities. He chooses to join the Night Watch, the division of the Light Others responsible for maintaining the balance between the supernatural and human worlds. As an operative, Anton battles Dark Others and uncovers a conspiracy that threatens the fragile equilibrium between the two sides, all while grappling with his own personal struggles and the complexities of power and morality within the supernatural society.\n(D) \"Grit\" by Angela Duckworth explores the concept of perseverance and passion in achieving long-term success, arguing that talent is not the key to success, but rather the combination of passion, resilience, and a growth mindset."}]}
{"id": 126, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book I Married a Communist. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"I Married a Communist\" is a novel by Jonathan Franzen that explores the life of a woman named Miriam Hopcraft, who marries a man named Philip St. Clair, an aspiring writer with communist leanings. The story spans several decades, delving into the complexities of their relationship, the impact of political ideologies on their lives, and the personal sacrifices they make. Through Miriam's perspective, the novel examines themes of love, loyalty, identity, and the consequences of choices made in the face of political turmoil. Franzen's novel also provides a critique of post-war America, societal expectations, and the lingering effects of the Red Scare on individual lives. \n(B) \"I Married a Communist\" is a novel by Jonathan Franzen that explores the life of a woman named Nan5 who becomes involved with a man named Richard, a former communist, and the impact of his past on their relationship and her life.\n(C) \"How to Be an Antiracist\" by Ibram X. Kendi is a guidebook that encourages readers to actively combat racism by examining their own beliefs and actions, and advocating for policies that promote equality and challenge systemic racism.\n(D) \"How to Be an Antiracist\" by Ibram X. Kendi is a guidebook that challenges readers to not only oppose racism but actively work towards becoming antiracist. Kendi argues that racial discrimination and inequality persist not only through individual prejudice but also through systemic policies and institutions. He encourages readers to examine their own beliefs and actions, acknowledging past complicity in upholding racism, and provides a framework for identifying and dismantling racist structures. The book emphasizes continuous self-reflection, education, and engagement in antiracist practices as essential components of personal and societal transformation."}]}
{"id": 127, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Intuitionist. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The Intuitionist is a novel by Colson Whitehead that explores themes of race, identity, and the nature of truth through the story of a black elevator inspector in 1970s America who subscribes to an alternative philosophy of elevator maintenance. \n(B) Reaper Man is a novel by Terry Pratchett, set in the Discworld series. It follows Death, the anthropomorphic personification of death, as he decides to take a vacation for the first time in centuries. In his absence, his assistant Kell assumes his duties and chaos ensues. Meanwhile, a local wizard, Death's friend William de Worde, becomes embroiled in a dispute between the living and the undead when a group of ghosts decide they don't want to be dead anymore. The story combines humor, philosophy, and adventure as Death learns the value of life, and the characters grapple with the concepts of mortality, change, and the importance of individual choice.\n(C) \"Reaper Man\" by Terry Pratchett is a humorous fantasy novel about Death learning to become a human and navigate life, while also dealing with a labor strike from his reaper minions and a dangerous soul collector.\n(D) \"The Intuitionist\" is a novel by Colson Whitehead that explores the life of Robert Lathrop, a black elevator inspector in 1970s New York City. Set against the backdrop of a philosophical debate between \"Intuitionists\" and \"Empiricists\" within the elevator industry, the story follows Lathrop's quest for professional validation and personal redemption, as he is accused of negligence after a tragic accident. The novel delves into themes of race, identity, systemic injustice, and the power of perception, while examining the深层次 implications of choosing faith over empirical evidence."}]}
{"id": 128, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Zone One. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Notes of a Native Son\" is a collection of essays by James Baldwin, first published in 1955. In these powerful and deeply personal writings, Baldwin explores the complexities of being a black man in America, delving into themes of race, identity, inequality, and the struggle for human dignity. Through his own experiences, as well as reflections on the lives of friends and prominent figures like his father and Malcolm X, Baldwin offers a searing critique of American society and its systemic racism. His eloquent prose serves as a call to action, urging readers to confront the harsh realities of racial injustice and to work towards a more just and understanding world. \n(B) Zone One is a post-apocalyptic novel by Colson Whitehead, set in a world where a zombie outbreak has decimated humanity. The story follows a消毒者（消毒者），a member of a cleanup crew, as he navigates through the abandoned streets of New York City, clearing out remaining undead. Along with two other survivors, Mark Spitz and执法者，they must confront not only the undead but also their own haunted pasts and the lingering questions about humanity in a world forever changed by the pandemic. Through their journey, Whitehead explores themes of memory, identity, and the human capacity for connection in the face of extreme adversity.\n(C) Zone One is a post-apocalyptic novel by Colson Whitehead, set in a world where a zombie outbreak has occurred, following a small group of survivors as they navigate through the abandoned streets of New York City.\n(D) Notes of a Native Son is a collection of essays by James Baldwin that explores his experiences as a black man in America, delving into themes of race, identity, and the complexities of the African American experience."}]}
{"id": 129, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Sympathizer. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Salem's Lot\" by Stephen King is a horror novel about a small town in Maine that becomes infested with vampires, focusing on a group of residents who fight to stop the vampire outbreak and save their loved ones. \n(B) The Sympathizer is a Pulitzer Prize-winning novel by Vietnamese-American author Viet Thanh Nguyen. It is a spy thriller and a literary commentary on the Vietnam War, told through the perspective of a communist double agent, known as the Sympathizer. The novel follows the story of the Sympathizer, a North Vietnamese captain, who infiltrates the South Vietnamese army during the final days of the war. As an undercover operative, he navigates the complex dynamics of loyalties, identity, and morality while grappling with his own conflicting feelings about the conflict. The novel explores themes of colonialism, imperialism, cultural displacement, and the psychological aftermath of war, offering a nuanced and critical perspective on both the Vietnamese and American experiences.\n(C) \"Salem's Lot\" is a horror novel by Stephen King, published in 1975. The story revolves around the small town of Salem's Lot in Maine, which becomes infested with vampires led by the charismatic and ancient vampire, Kurt Barlow. The narrative follows two main characters: Ben Mears, a writer who returns to the town, and Father Louis Deschain, a priest who becomes aware of the supernatural occurrences. As the townspeople are turned into vampires, Ben and Louis form an unlikely alliance to uncover the truth and save the town from eternal darkness. The novel explores themes of fear, isolation, and the battle between good and evil, with a haunting atmosphere and chilling encounters.\n(D) The Sympathizer is a novel by Viet Thanh Nguyen that tells the story of a communist spy working undercover as an aide to a South Vietnamese general during and after the Vietnam War, exploring themes of identity, loyalty, and the complex aftermath of the conflict."}]}
{"id": 130, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Sweet Tooth. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Sweet Tooth is a post-apocalyptic fantasy comic book series created by Jeff Lemire. Set in a world where a mysterious disease has caused most of humanity to turn into animal-human hybrid creatures, the story follows Gus, a young boy with deer-like antlers, who is one of the last members of his hybrid community. He embarks on a journey to find the legendary \"Safe Zone\" while encountering various survivors, both friend and foe, along the way. The series explores themes of identity, belonging, and the resilience of humanity in the face of adversity, all told through a beautifully illustrated and emotionally captivating narrative. \n(B) Sweet Tooth is a post-apocalyptic fantasy comic book series that follows a young boy with deer-like antlers, as he embarks on a journey to discover the truth about his origins and the mysterious world around him.\n(C) Illywhacker is a novel by Australian author Peter Carey that follows the life and adventures of a charismatic con man named Albert \"Illywhacker\" Spence, weaving together elements of magical realism, family history, and Australian folklore.\n(D) Illywhacker is a novel by Australian author Peter Carey, published in 1985. The complex and multilayered narrative follows the life of Albert \"Albie\"注昂利\"艾尔比\"Thornhill, a charismatic and unreliable narrator who moves through various identities and professions, including a door-to-door salesman, a con artist, and a writer. Set against the backdrop of 20th-century Australia, the novel weaves together elements of magical realism, family history, and the country's cultural identity, exploring themes of storytelling, reinvention, and the search for meaning in a rapidly changing world. Illywhacker is known for its intricate plot, rich characterizations, and Carey's masterful use of language, offering a satirical take on Australian society and the human condition."}]}
{"id": 131, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Japanese Lover. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Japanese Lover\" by Lisa See is a novel that spans generations and cultures, following the lives of a Japanese-American woman and her enduring love story with a Chinese man, exploring themes of identity, war, family, and the power of connection. \n(B) Life After Life, a novel by Kate Atkinson, follows the multiple lives of Ursula Todd, a woman who experiences the possibility of reliving her life upon death, making different choices and observing the蝴蝶 effect on her personal life and the historical events surrounding her, particularly during World War II. Through various timelines, Ursula navigates love, loss, and the power of free will, ultimately seeking to understand her purpose and find a way to alter the course of history for the better.\n(C) \"The Japanese Lover\" is a novel by Richard Powers that tells the story of a complex and enduring love triangle spanning several decades. The narrative revolves around the life of丈, a Japanese man who falls deeply in love with a young European refugee, Hana. Their relationship is tested by war, separation, and personal challenges, leading Hana to make choices that impact their lives and those of the people around them, including Hana's daughter, Nicole. Through generations, the themes of love, loss, resilience, and the enduring power of connection are explored as Nicole uncovers her mother's past and grapples with her own romantic relationships. The novel weaves together history, cultural differences, and the enduring nature of love across generations.\n(D) Life After Life by Kate Atkinson is a novel about a woman named Ursula Todd who experiences multiple lifetimes, exploring the concept of reincarnation and the impact of small changes on the course of history."}]}
{"id": 132, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Veronika Decides to Die. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Veronika Decides to Die is a novel about a young woman who, after a failed suicide attempt, finds a new sense of purpose and meaning in life while被困 in a mental institution. \n(B) Kitchen Confidential is a memoir by chef Anthony Bourdain, published in 2000, in which he offers a no-holds-barred insider's look at the restaurant industry. Bourdain candidly describes his journey from a young, rebellious cook to a successful chef, exposing the gritty, unsanitized realities of life in the kitchen. He shares stories of drug use, infidelity, cutthroat competition, and the intense camaraderie among culinary professionals. The book not only serves as a tell-all exposé but also celebrates the passion and drive that fuels the people who work in the high-pressure world of professional cooking. Kitchen Confidential became a bestseller and established Bourdain as a prominent culinary voice, leading to his television career and further explorations of food culture.\n(C) Kitchen Confidential is a memoir by Anthony Bourdain in which he shares his experiences and insights from working in professional kitchens.\n(D) Veronika Decides to Die, written by Paulo Coelho, is a novel about a young woman named Veronika who, disillusioned with life, decides to end her existence in a suicide attempt. She survives and finds herself in a mental institution, where she initially plans to escape. However, as she interacts with the eccentric and genuine patients, Veronika begins to question her previous views on happiness and the meaning of life. Through her experiences and relationships within the institution, Veronika learns to appreciate the beauty of life's imperfections and discovers her own inner strength, ultimately choosing to live a more authentic and fulfilling existence."}]}
{"id": 133, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book David Golder. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) David Golder is a fictional character created by French author Irène Némirovsky. He is the protagonist in Némirovsky's novel \"David Golder,\" published in 1929. David Golder is a wealthy Jewish financier living in Paris during the interwar period. The story revolves around his life, his relationships, particularly with his daughter, and his struggle to maintain his power and wealth amidst personal crises and the changing socio-economic landscape. Golder's character embodies the themes of wealth, family dynamics, and the complexities of identity in a world on the brink of significant change. \n(B) \"Petals of Blood\" by Ngugi wa Thiong'o is a collection of four interconnected novels that expose and criticize the neo-colonialism, corruption, and social inequalities in post-independent Kenya.\n(C) \"Petals of Blood\" is a collection of four interconnected novellas by Kenyan author Ngugi wa Thiong'o, published in 1977. The works explore the themes of colonialism, post-colonialism, corruption, and the struggle for social justice in Kenya. Through the stories of ordinary individuals, such as a peasant, a schoolteacher, a doctor, and a writer, the author portrays the harsh realities and systemic oppression faced by the Kenyan people. The novellas serve as a powerful critique of neocolonialism, capitalism, and the betrayal of the nation's ideals by those in power, while also offering glimpses of hope and the resilience of the human spirit.\n(D) David Golder is a novel by French author Irène Némirovsky,讲述了一个犹太银行家的悲剧性生活故事，反映了财富、社会地位与人性的冲突。"}]}
{"id": 134, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book A Hunger Artist. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Blink is a book by Malcolm Gladwell that explores the power of intuitive decision-making and how our subconscious minds can make quick, accurate judgments based on subtle patterns and information. \n(B) A Hunger Artist is a short story by Franz Kafka about a man who makes a living as a fasting performer. He becomes renowned for his ability to go without food for long periods, attracting large crowds who come to watch his displays of self-denial. However, the artist becomes increasingly dissatisfied with the commercialization of his act and longs for a more authentic, spiritual experience of fasting. Despite his desire for a deeper connection with his audience, he is reduced to a spectacle, eventually fasting to the point of death, which is met with little understanding or empathy from the public. The story explores themes of artistic integrity, human spectacle, and the futility of seeking genuine emotional connection in a society driven by entertainment and consumerism.\n(C) Blink is a book written by Malcolm Gladwell that explores the power of rapid cognition and the role it plays in decision-making. Gladwell argues that sometimes our intuitive, instinctive decisions can be more accurate and effective than those made through thorough analysis. He delves into the science behind \"thin-slicing,\" the ability to make snap judgments based on limited information, and examines various case studies, from criminal investigations to business strategies, to demonstrate the advantages and limitations of relying on our gut instincts. The book challenges conventional wisdom on the importance of deliberation and encourages readers to appreciate the value of intuitive thinking in a world that often prioritizes data-driven reasoning.\n(D) \"A Hunger Artist\" is a short story by Franz Kafka about a performer who achieves fame for his ability to go without food for long periods, but ultimately feels unappreciated and isolated in his art."}]}
{"id": 135, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Wonderful, Wonderful Times. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Man Who Died Twice\" is a novel by Anthony Horowitz, the second installment in the \"Artemis Fowl\" series. It continues the adventures of the young criminal mastermind Artemis Fowl, who, after his previous encounter with fairies, finds himself entangled in a new mystery. When Artemis's father goes missing, believed to be kidnapped by the Russian mafia, Artemis assembles a team to rescue him. However, the mission takes an unexpected turn when they discover that Artemis Sr. has a secret past involving a powerful and dangerous object. Alongside fairy allies and new characters, Artemis must navigate a web of deception, international espionage, and supernatural elements to save his father and confront his own identity. \n(B) \"The Man Who Died Twice\" is a novel by Anthony Horowitz, which continues the story of Daniel Portman, a former spy turned private investigator, as he becomes entangled in a dangerous conspiracy involving a deadly virus and a mysterious villain from his past.\n(C) \"Wonderful, Wonderful Times\" is a novel by Taiwanese author Pai Hsien-yung that explores the lives of a group of high school friends in Taiwan during the 1950s and 1960s, against the backdrop of political turmoil and social change. The story delves into their friendships, romances, and personal struggles as they navigate the complexities of adolescence, family expectations, and the restrictive societal norms of the time. Through their experiences, the novel portrays the universal themes of love, loss, and the pursuit of individuality amidst political repression and cultural conformity.\n(D) \"Wonderful, Wonderful Times\" is a novel by Taiwanese author Pai Hsien-yung that explores the complex relationships and personal struggles of a group of friends during Taiwan's turbulent post-war period."}]}
{"id": 136, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Seiobo There Below. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Bread and Wine\" is a memoir by Francine Pascal that explores her experiences with food, cooking, and the connections it creates among family and friends. \n(B) Seiobo There Below is a novel by Japanese author Yoko Tawada that weaves together elements of mythology, history, and contemporary life. The narrative follows Seiobo, a deity from Chinese mythology, as he observes and interacts with various characters throughout different time periods and geographical locations. Tawada explores themes of impermanence, the interconnectedness of humanity, and the relationship between nature and civilization. The book combines poetic language, magical realism, and philosophical musings, creating a dreamlike and thought-provoking exploration of the human condition and our place in the world.\n(C) \"Bread and Wine\" is a memoir by Francine Pascal that explores her journey through the ups and downs of marriage, motherhood, and the complexities of family life. Through a series of interconnected essays, Pascal delves into the challenges and joys of raising children, maintaining a long-term relationship, and finding personal fulfillment while navigating the expectations of society. With humor and honesty, she reflects on the power of food and shared meals in bringing people together, as well as the transformative nature of love, loss, and the passage of time. \"Bread and Wine\" is a poignant and relatable reflection on the complexities of modern family life and the importance of cherishing the moments that truly matter.\n(D) Seiobo There Below is a novel by Japanese author Yoko Tawada that weaves together stories and myths from different cultures, exploring the relationship between humanity, nature, and the divine, through the perspective of the ancient deity Seiobo."}]}
{"id": 137, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Silent House. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Silent House is a 2011 psychological horror film that follows the story of a young woman named Sarah, who becomes trapped in her family's remote vacation home with a mysterious intruder. As the night progresses, she experiences unexplainable events and terrifying encounters, leading her to question her own sanity while trying to survive the night and uncover the dark secrets hidden within the house. The film is notable for its unique selling point of being presented in one continuous shot, creating a sense of intense realism and immersion for the audience. \n(B) Silent House is a horror film that follows a young woman as she becomes trapped in her childhood home, which begins to change and haunt her, revealing a dark secret from the past.\n(C) Red Queen is a dystopian young adult novel by Victoria Aveyard, set in a world where people are divided into two classes based on their ability to manipulate elemental forces. The story follows Mare Barrow, a \"common\" girl from the poverty-stricken Reds, who discovers she possesses a rare power, making her a \"Silver.\" This revelation thrusts Mare into the luxurious yet treacherous court of the Silvers, where she becomes embroiled in a dangerous conspiracy and a rebellion against the oppressive ruling class. As she grapples with her newfound identity and the weight of her powers, Mare must choose between loyalty to her people and survival in a society that seeks to control her.\n(D) Red Queen is a dystopian novel by Victoria Aveyard, set in a world where people with red blood have low status, while those with silver blood have special powers, and follows the story of Mare Barrow, a red-blooded girl who discovers she has hidden powers and becomes caught in a rebellion against the ruling silver elite."}]}
{"id": 138, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Cairo Trilogy. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Hotel du Lac is a novel by British author Anita Brookner, published in 1984. It tells the story of Edith Hope, a middle-aged Englishwoman who retreats to the Swiss hotel of the title to escape her complicated personal life in London. There, she becomes entangled with the other guests, particularly the enigmatic Mr. Mazzini, and grapples with her own desires, disappointments, and the choices she has made. Through Edith's introspective journey, the novel explores themes of love, loneliness, morality, and the search for self-understanding. \n(B) The Cairo Trilogy is a series of three novels by Egyptian author Naguib Mahfouz, consisting of \"Children of the Nile,\" \"The Road of Love,\" and \" Palace of Desire.\" Set in Cairo during the early 20th century, the epic family saga follows the lives of the patriarch, Al-Sayyid Ahmad Abd al-Jawad, his wife Amina, and their children over three generations. The novels explore themes of tradition, modernity, love, betrayal, and the changing roles of Egyptian society, particularly focusing on the experiences of women within the family and the broader context of a nation undergoing political and social transformation. Through its richly drawn characters and intricate narrative, the trilogy provides a profound insight into the complexities of Egyptian culture and the human condition.\n(C) Hotel du Lac is a novel by British author Anita Brookner, which tells the story of a reclusive writer who spends a transformative summer at a Swiss hotel, reflecting on her past and forming unexpected relationships.\n(D) The Cairo Trilogy is a novel by Naguib Mahfouz that follows the lives of three generations of an Egyptian family in Cairo, exploring themes of tradition, modernity, and personal identity against the backdrop of political and social change in the early 20th century."}]}
{"id": 139, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Storyteller of Marrakesh. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Children of Blood and Bone is a young adult fantasy novel that follows the story of Zélie, a young girl in a world where magic has been suppressed, as she joins a revolution to bring it back and reclaim her people's rightful place. \n(B) \"The Storyteller of Marrakesh\" is a novel by Moroccan author Tahar Ben Jelloun, which weaves together the lives of two men from different backgrounds. The protagonist, a successful writer, becomes fascinated with a local storyteller in the vibrant city of Marrakesh. As the writer listens to the storyteller's captivating tales, he becomes entangled in a web of love, tradition, and political intrigue. Through their encounters, the novel explores themes of identity, cultural preservation, and the power of storytelling to bridge generational and societal divides. It also delves into the complexities of modern Morocco, shedding light on its rich history and the challenges faced by its people.\n(C) Children of Blood and Bone is a young adult fantasy novel by Tomi Adeyemi, set in the African-inspired world of Orisha. It follows the story of Zélie, a young girl who lives in a kingdom where magic has been suppressed by an oppressive government. After her brother is murdered, Zélie joins forces with a group of rebels, including a prodigious mage and a troubled prince, on a quest to restore magic and bring hope back to their people. The novel explores themes of identity, revolution, and the power of love and sacrifice, as the characters navigate a dangerous journey filled with magic, betrayal, and the resilience of the human spirit.\n(D) \"The Storyteller of Marrakesh\" is a novel that intertwines the lives of a young writer and a mysterious storyteller in the vibrant city of Marrakesh, exploring themes of love, tradition, and the power of narrative."}]}
{"id": 140, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Long Walk to Freedom. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Why Zebras Don't Get Ulcers\" by Robert M. Sapolsky is a book that explores the effects of stress on the human body and mind, offering insights into stress physiology and ways to manage it. \n(B) \"The Long Walk to Freedom\" is Nelson Mandela's autobiographical account of his life, chronicling his journey from his childhood in rural South Africa to his role as a prominent anti-apartheid activist, his 27-year imprisonment, and his eventual emergence as South Africa's first black president. The book not only recounts Mandela's personal struggles and triumphs, but also provides a historical perspective on the fight against apartheid, the inner workings of the African National Congress, and Mandela's vision for a democratic and unified South Africa. It is a powerful story of resilience, leadership, and the triumph of the human spirit over adversity.\n(C) \"Why Zebras Don't Get Ulcers\" by Robert M. Sapolsky is a book that explores the concept of stress and its impact on human health. Sapolsky uses a combination of scientific research and engaging anecdotes to explain the physiological and psychological mechanisms behind stress responses, drawing comparisons to the way other animals cope with stress in the wild. He discusses the harmful effects of chronic stress on the body, including its role in various diseases, and offers insights into effective stress management strategies. The book aims to educate readers on the nature of stress and equip them with tools to better navigate and reduce its negative consequences in their lives.\n(D) \"The Long Walk to Freedom\" is Nelson Mandela's autobiography, chronicling his life from childhood through his anti-apartheid activism, imprisonment, and ultimately his presidency in South Africa."}]}
{"id": 141, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book A Bend in the River. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Explosion in a Cathedral\" is a short story by Gabriel Garcia Marquez that explores political intrigue, love, and the power of memory, set against the backdrop of a fictional Latin American country. \n(B) A Bend in the River, written by V.S. Naipaul, is a novel that explores the complex and devastating effects of colonialism and its aftermath in an unnamed African country. Through the eyes of the narrator, a brown-skinned Indian businessman named Wainwright, the story delves into the intertwined histories of European exploitation, native resistance, and the lingering impact on the indigenous population. As Wainwright witnesses the gradual disintegration of a once-thriving trading post and the brutal conflicts that arise, the novel grapples with themes of cultural erosion, personal identity, and the destructive power of greed and ideology, painting a haunting portrait of a region caught in the crosshairs of history.\n(C) A Bend in the River is a novel by V.S. Naipaul that explores the complex and devastating impact of colonialism and its aftermath on a fictional African country, through the eyes of a brown Indian narrator.\n(D) \"Explosion in a Cathedral\" is a short story by Gabriel Garcia Marquez, first published in 1969. It is set in an unnamed Latin American city and revolves around the assassination attempt on a bishop during his visit to a cathedral. The explosion, caused by a hidden bomb, kills several people but miraculously spares the bishop. The story explores themes of political turmoil, religious symbolism, and the impact of violence on a community. It delves into the intricate relationships between the characters, including a secret love affair, and the power dynamics that exist within the city's elite and the oppressed. Through magical realism, Marquez weaves a tale that transcends the physical event, reflecting on the deeper emotional and psychological consequences of violence and the resilience of the human spirit."}]}
{"id": 142, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Mystic Masseur. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The Riddle-Master of Hed is a high fantasy novel by Patricia A. McKillip, first published in 1976. It follows the journey of a young man named Garion, known as the Riddle-Master, as he quests to unlock the secrets of powerful magical objects called the Seven jeweled Riddles. Set in the mystical land of Hed, Garion encounters various mystical creatures, allies, and enemies, while unraveling riddles and facing formidable foes. His quest not only tests his intellect and courage but also explores themes of destiny, friendship, and the struggle between good and evil. The novel is the first installment in the Riddle-Master trilogy, which continues Garion's story as he learns more about his own heritage and the ancient forces at play in his world. \n(B) \"The Mystic Masseur\" is a novel by V.S. Naipaul that tells the story of Ganesh Ramsumair, a Trinidadian man who, in search of a livelihood, reinvents himself as a spiritual healer and mystic. Through his newfound persona, he gains popularity and success, navigating the complexities of Trinidad's multicultural society, colonial influences, and his own personal desires and shortcomings. The novel explores themes of identity, fraud, and the power of storytelling, as Ganesh's charade forces him to confront the blurred lines between reality and illusion.\n(C) The Riddle-Master of Hed is a fantasy novel by Patricia A. McKillip, following the journey of a young man named Garion as he unravels riddles and confronts dark forces to protect a powerful artifact in a magical world.\n(D) \"The Mystic Masseur\" by VS Naipaul is a satirical novel about a man in Trinidad who becomes a spiritual leader and healer, exploring themes of identity, culture, and colonialism."}]}
{"id": 143, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Essays in Idleness. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Oblomov is a novel by Ivan Goncharov that follows the lazy and unambitious protagonist, Oblomov, as he struggles with his apathy and inability to act, while his friend Stolz tries to motivate him to engage with life. \n(B) Oblomov is a novel by Russian author Ivan Goncharov, published in 1859. It tells the story of the lazy and unambitious protagonist, Ivan Oblomov, who spends his days lounging in his bed and avoiding any kind of activity or social engagement. Set in Moscow, the novel explores Oblomov's contrasting lifestyle with that of his active and enterprising friend, Stolz, who tries to motivate him to improve his life. Through their interactions, the novel delves into themes of idleness, the decline of the Russian aristocracy, and the struggle between tradition and change during the mid-19th century. Oblomov has become a symbol of procrastination and the inability to act, with his character serving as a critique of the apathetic Russian society of the time.\n(C) \"Essays in Idleness,\" also known as \"Tsurezuregusa\" in Japanese, is a collection of philosophical and literary musings by Japanese author and courtier, Kenko Mumon (1283-1350). The text, written in a闲 (tsurezure) or leisurely style, consists of brief, introspective essays that explore a wide range of topics, including Zen Buddhism, aesthetics, social customs, nature, and the transitory nature of life. Kenko's witty and often cynical observations provide insights into the complexities of medieval Japanese society, while also delving into universal themes of human existence, wisdom, and the pursuit of happiness in an imperfect world. The work is considered a masterpiece of Japanese literature and a significant contribution to the development of the boshetsu (miscellaneous prose) genre.\n(D) \"Essays in Idleness\" is a collection of philosophical and humorous reflections on Japanese culture and life, written by the 13th-century nobleman and Buddhist monk, Yoshida Kenkō."}]}
{"id": 144, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Nakano Thrift Shop. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The Nakano Thrift Shop is a novel by Hiromi Kawakami that follows the lives of two introverted siblings who work at their family's thrift store in Tokyo, exploring themes of loneliness, connection, and the beauty found in everyday life. \n(B) \"The Nakano Thrift Shop\" is a novel by Japanese author Hiromi Kawakami that follows the lives of three introverted siblings living in Tokyo. The siblings find solace in their shared love for literature and music, as they work at their family's thrift shop, which becomes a haven for their quiet existence. The story revolves around their平淡 yet emotionally rich lives, as they navigate relationships, dreams, and the simple joys found in their daily routines, highlighting the beauty in the mundane.\n(C) In a Free State is a novel by V.S. Naipaul that explores the themes of identity, colonialism, and political turmoil through the experiences of a group of expatriates in an unnamed African country. The story follows the lives of four main characters: a Trinidadian writer, a white South African, a black African academic, and a British diplomat, as they navigate their personal relationships and the increasingly unstable political climate. Naipaul delves into the complexities of race, power dynamics, and the lingering effects of colonialism, while also examining the struggle for individual freedom and the search for a sense of belonging in a world marked by cultural dislocation and political upheaval.\n(D) In a Free State is a novel by V.S. Naipaul that explores the complexities of race, politics, and identity through the experiences of a group of expatriates in a fictional African country."}]}
{"id": 145, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Ball Lightning. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Ball lightning, also known as plasma balls or globes, is a rare and poorly understood atmospheric electrical phenomenon characterized by a floating, luminous, spherical entity that appears during thunderstorms. Its appearance varies, often ranging from small to large sizes, and it exhibits a range of colors, typically white, yellow, orange, or blue. The exact nature of ball lightning remains elusive, with theories suggesting it could be a result of electrical discharge, plasma formations, or chemical reactions. Observations are scarce and inconsistent, making it challenging for scientists to study and fully comprehend this elusive natural wonder. \n(B) Ball lightning is a rare and poorly understood atmospheric electrical phenomenon表现为发光的球体，通常在雷暴期间出现，其性质和成因至今仍存在许多争议。\n(C) Babel Tower is a thought-provoking and imaginative novel by奉俊昊 (奉俊昊), a South Korean author. The story revolves around the construction of a colossal tower in modern-day Korea, reminiscent of the biblical Tower of Babel. This ambitious project aims to reach the heavens, uniting people from diverse backgrounds and languages in a quest for transcendence and progress. However, as the tower grows taller, it becomes a microcosm of societal issues, power struggles, and the inherent complexities of human communication. Through its intricate narrative and exploration of themes like identity, ambition, and the consequences of technological advancement, Babel Tower offers a captivating commentary on humanity's eternal yearning for connection and the barriers that prevent it.\n(D) Babel Tower is a game where players collaborate to build a tower by stacking blocks while communicating in a shared but randomly assigned language, emphasizing the challenges and乐趣 of language barriers."}]}
{"id": 146, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Scarlatti Inheritance. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Scarlatti Inheritance\" is a novel by Robert Ludlum that follows an archaeologist's quest to uncover a hidden fortune linked to the Scarlatti family, entangled with danger, conspiracy, and the secrets of ancient Rome. \n(B) The Voyage of the Dawn Treader is a children's fantasy novel by C.S. Lewis, in which four siblings embark on a magical journey aboard a ship, encountering mythical creatures and learning valuable life lessons.\n(C) The Voyage of the Dawn Treader is a children's fantasy novel by C.S. Lewis, the third book in The Chronicles of Narnia series. It follows the adventures of Edmund, Lucy, and their cousin Eustace, who are whisked away to the magical world of Narnia aboard the ship Dawn Treader. Along with King Caspian, they embark on a quest to explore mysterious islands, encounter mythical creatures, battle evil forces, and discover the significance of courage, selflessness, and true kingship. Through their journey, the children learn valuable life lessons and are ultimately transformed by their experiences in Narnia.\n(D) The Scarlatti Inheritance is a novel by British author T. J. Baker, which revolves around the mysterious and wealthy Scarlatti family. When the last direct descendant of the family dies under suspicious circumstances, a group of distant relatives, each with their own secrets and motives, gather at the family's sprawling Italian villa. As they navigate a complex web of inheritance, betrayal, and long-held grudges, they soon realize that the Scarlatti fortune comes with a dangerous legacy. Uncovering the truth behind the family's dark past, hidden treasures, and a series of cryptic clues becomes a race against time, as the relatives must confront their own demons and confront the possibility that one among them may be willing to kill to claim the inheritance."}]}
{"id": 147, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Guns of Navarone. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"A Fall of Moondust\" is a science fiction novel by Arthur C. Clarke, published in 1961. The story revolves around a group of astronauts who become trapped in a massive dust cloud on the moon, leading to a thrilling and challenging rescue mission. As the survivors fight against time, limited resources, and the unknown dangers of the dust cloud, they also uncover ancient lunar secrets and face ethical dilemmas. Through vivid descriptions and scientific plausibility, Clarke explores themes of human resilience, the mysteries of the universe, and the potential for extraterrestrial life. \n(B) \"A Fall of Moondust\" by Arthur C. Clarke is a science fiction novel about a group of astronauts who become trapped in a dust cloud on the moon and their struggle for survival and rescue.\n(C) \"The Guns of Navarone\" is a gripping World War II adventure novel by Alistair MacLean, in which a small group of Allied commandos is tasked with a seemingly impossible mission: to infiltrate the heavily fortified Greek island of Navarone and destroy the German artillery guns threatening Allied shipping. Led by Captain Keith Mallory and Sergeant Major Miller, the team must overcome numerous obstacles, enemy patrols, and treacherous terrain, while also dealing with internal conflicts and unexpected alliances. The novel combines intense action, suspenseful plot twists, and vivid descriptions of war-torn landscapes, making it a classic in the military fiction genre.\n(D) The Guns of Navarone is a World War II thriller novel about a group of Allied commandos who embark on a dangerous mission to destroy German artillery guns threatening a crucial Mediterranean island."}]}
{"id": 148, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Before I Go to Sleep. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Before I Go to Sleep is a psychological thriller novel by S.J. Watson. The story revolves around Christine Lucas, a woman suffering from anterograde amnesia, a condition that prevents her from forming new memories. Each day, she wakes up with no recollection of her past, her husband, or even her own identity. Her life is a continuous loop of forgetting and relearning, as she relies on journal entries and a voice recording to piece together her life. As Christine starts to question the truth about her past and her husband's motives, she delves into a dark and dangerous mystery, uncovering shocking secrets that threaten her life. The novel explores themes of memory, identity, and the reliability of perception, keeping readers on the edge of their seats until the surprising revelation of the truth. \n(B) Shuggie Bain is a novel by Douglas Stuart that tells the story of a young boy growing up in 1980s Glasgow, Scotland. Shuggie is the youngest child of an alcoholic mother and a distant father, and faces a challenging upbringing surrounded by poverty, domestic violence, and social unrest. The novel explores themes of family, addiction, sexuality, and the power of resilience as Shuggie navigates his complex relationships and struggles to find his place in a harsh and unforgiving world. Through Shuggie's experiences, Stuart paints a vivid and poignant portrait of working-class life and the enduring impact of love and loss.\n(C) Shuggie Bain is a novel by Douglas Stuart that tells the story of a young boy growing up in 1980s Glasgow, Scotland, as he navigates poverty, family dysfunction, and his own emerging sexuality while trying to find his place in the world.\n(D) \"Before I Go to Sleep\" is a psychological thriller novel by S.J. Watson, in which a woman with anterograde amnesia wakes up every day not knowing who she is or why she can't remember her past."}]}
{"id": 149, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Thursday Murder Club. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Eye of the Storm\" is a novel by Australian author Patrick White, published in 1973. It tells the story of SybyllaFFirbank, a wealthy and eccentric matriarch in her eighties, who, despite her declining health, still exerts a powerful influence over her family and the people around her. As she awaits the arrival of her estranged daughter and granddaughter, Sybylla reflects on her life,爱丽丝回忆起她的一生,回顾了她的过去，审视了她的选择和错过的爱情。小说通过Sybylla的眼睛，探讨了衰老、记忆、家庭关系以及个人与社会变迁的深刻主题。\"The Eye of the Storm\" is a powerful exploration of the human condition and a meditation on the complexities of a life lived to the fullest. \n(B) The Thursday Murder Club is a novel by Richard Osman, following a group of four elderly friends living in a retirement community called Coopers Hill. On Thursdays, they meet to discuss unsolved crimes, dubbing themselves the Thursday Murder Club. When a body is discovered on the construction site of a new development in their village, the club members find themselves inadvertently involved in solving the real-life mystery. With their unique skills, keen观察力, and access to the community's gossip, they uncover secrets, navigate personal relationships, and ultimately try to bring the killer to justice, all while managing the challenges of their golden years.\n(C) \"The Eye of the Storm\" is a novel by Patrick White that follows the life of a wealthy and controversial matriarch, Sybylla Vincent, and her family during a reunion amidst her declining health.\n(D) The Thursday Murder Club is a novel by Richard Osman where a group of退休朋友们在他们的英国退休社区解决谋杀案。"}]}
{"id": 150, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Tears of the Giraffe. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Tears of the Giraffe\" is a novel by Karin Fossum that follows a Norwegian police inspector as he investigates the mysterious death of a young boy in a small African country, uncovering corruption and political intrigue along the way. \n(B) Juneteenth, also known as Freedom Day or Emancipation Day, is an annual holiday in the United States commemorating the emancipation of enslaved African Americans. It originated in Texas on June 19, 1865, when Union General Gordon Granger announced the end of slavery, two and a half years after the signing of the Emancipation Proclamation. The holiday celebrates freedom, cultural heritage, and the ongoing fight for equality, with various festivities, educational events, and gatherings held across the country. In 2021, Juneteenth was recognized as a federal holiday, acknowledging its significance in American history.\n(C) Juneteenth is an annual holiday commemorating the emancipation of enslaved African Americans in the United States, celebrated on June 19th.\n(D) \"Tears of the Giraffe\" is a novel by British author Alexander McCall Smith, set in the fictional African country of Botswana. It is the second book in the popular No. 1 Ladies' Detective Agency series, featuring protagonist Precious Ramotswe, the first female private detective in Gaborone. The story follows Precious as she investigates various cases, including a suspicious insurance claim and a missing husband, while navigating the complexities of life in Botswana. With its gentle humor, vivid portrayal of African culture, and emphasis on morality and human kindness, \"Tears of the Giraffe\" offers a heartwarming and insightful tale, blending elements of detective fiction with social commentary."}]}
{"id": 151, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Nigger of the Narcissus. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The Night Circus is a captivating novel by Erin Morgenstern that weaves a spellbinding tale of enchantment, rivalry, and love. Set in the late 19th century, it follows the lives of two young illusionists, Celia and Marco, who are reluctantly pitted against each other in a mysterious and enchanting traveling circus called Le Cirque des Rêves. The circus, which only operates at night, becomes a mesmerizing battleground as Celia and Marco create extraordinary attractions, each vying to outdo the other without knowing the true purpose of their competition. As their talents and feelings intertwine, they must confront the hidden forces manipulating their fate while navigating the magical world of the Night Circus, where dreams and reality blur. \n(B) The Night Circus is a magical novel about a mysterious, enchanting circus that appears only at night and hosts a captivating competition between two young illusionists, set against the backdrop of turn-of-the-century Europe.\n(C) \"The Nigger of the Narcissus\" is a novel by Joseph Conrad, first published in 1897. The story revolves around a diverse crew on the merchant ship Narcissus, focusing on an enigmatic black sailor named Jim, who initially serves as a symbol of racial and social tensions among the crew. As the voyage progresses, Jim's mysterious past and complex character challenge the船上 crew's prejudices and perceptions. A series of unfortunate events leads to Jim's perceived contamination, resulting in his isolation and eventual death. Through this narrative, Conrad explores themes of race, identity, humanity, and the destructive power of prejudice, set against the backdrop of the vast, indifferent sea.\n(D) \"The Nigger of the Narcissus\" is a novel by Joseph Conrad that explores themes of race, identity, and morality through the experiences of a diverse crew on a merchant ship."}]}
{"id": 152, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Virtual Light. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) One Man's Bible is a novel by James Lee Burke that explores the intersecting lives of a biblically obsessed serial killer and a detective in Louisiana, delving into themes of faith, violence, and the human condition. \n(B) Virtual Light is a science fiction novel by William Gibson, published in 1993. Set in a near-future San Francisco, the story follows the interconnected lives of various characters, including a washed-up rock musician, a hacker, and a former police officer. The plot revolves around a mysterious artifact with powerful technological capabilities, which becomes the center of a struggle between different factions. The novel explores themes of artificial intelligence, virtual reality, corporate greed, and the blurred lines between the physical and digital worlds, showcasing Gibson's signature \"cyberpunk\" style and prescient predictions of future technologies.\n(C) One Man's Bible is a novel by Canadian author Timothy Findley that explores the complex relationship between religion, personal faith, and the power of literature. The story follows a widowed Anglican priest named Richard Bennis, who becomes obsessed with annotating and rewriting his personal copy of the Bible. As he delves deeper into the text, he questions institutional dogma, grapples with his own sexuality, and confronts the shadows of his past. Through Bennis' journey, Findley invites readers to reconsider the sacred text as a dynamic, interpretive work shaped by individual perspectives and the human experience. The novel is a profound meditation on the nature of belief, the search for meaning, and the enduring power of the written word.\n(D) Virtual Light is a cyberpunk novel by William Gibson, set in a near-future San Francisco, that follows a hacker and a ex-cop as they investigate a series of mysterious events connected to a virtual reality device."}]}
{"id": 153, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Redshirts. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Pillars of the Earth\" is a historical novel by Ken Follett, set in 12th-century England. It follows the lives of several characters, including stonemason Tom Builder, his family, and the ambitious priest William Hamleigh, as they become entangled in the building of a magnificent cathedral in the fictional town of Kingsbridge. The story explores themes of power, love, politics, and the struggle for survival, all against the backdrop of the turbulent times, including the conflict between King Stephen and Empress Matilda, the rise of the Gothic architecture, and the impact of the church on society. Through their trials and triumphs, the characters' stories interweave, highlighting the human cost and the enduring legacy of the cathedral's construction. \n(B) Redshirts is a science fiction novel by John Scalzi, published in 2012. The story follows a group of low-ranking crew members on a spaceship called the USS Enterprise-D (a nod to Star Trek), who begin to suspect that their seemingly routine missions are part of a sinister plot. They discover that they are expendable \"redshirts\" in a narrative cycle, destined to die to save the lives of the main characters. The characters, led by the protagonist John Perry, start to subvert these expectations, fighting against the story's predetermined fate and uncovering the truth behind their reality. The novel is a humorous and metafictional exploration of science fiction tropes, while also delving into themes of free will, storytelling, and the power of the narrative.\n(C) \"The Pillars of the Earth\" is a historical novel by Ken Follett that follows the construction of a cathedral in medieval England and the lives of the people involved, exploring power, love, and corruption against the backdrop of societal upheaval.\n(D) \"Redshirts\" by John Scalzi is a science fiction novel that satirizes tropes and conventions of the genre, following a group of low-ranking crew members on a spaceship who start to suspect that their roles are predetermined and dangerous."}]}
{"id": 154, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Pushing Ice. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Pushing Ice\" by Alastair Reynolds is a science fiction novel about a former police officer who becomes involved in a dangerous mission to investigate an alien artifact discovered in the Oort Cloud. \n(B) \"Berlin: The Downfall 1945\" is a historical account by Antony Beevor that provides a detailed and harrowing examination of the final days of World War II in Berlin. The book chronicles the brutal Soviet siege and the devastating battle that ensued, culminating in the city's fall to the红军. It delves into the experiences of soldiers and civilians alike, depicting the horrors of war, widespread atrocities, and the collapse of the Nazi regime. Beevor masterfully weaves together personal narratives, military strategies, and political machinations to create a gripping and poignant portrayal of one of history's darkest moments.\n(C) \"Berlin: The Downfall 1945\" is a historical account that narrates the final days of World War II in Berlin, focusing on the brutal battle between the Soviet红军 and the German Wehrmacht, leading to the city's capture and the end of Adolf Hitler's reign.\n(D) \"Pushing Ice\" is a hard science fiction novel by Alastair Reynolds, set in a future where humanity has colonized the outer reaches of the solar system. The story follows Hilda Lim, an ice miner on a massive ship tasked with redirecting comets towards Earth to alleviate its water crisis. When the ship stumbles upon an alien artifact in the Oort Cloud, Hilda becomes entangled in a mystery that could have dire consequences for humanity. As they journey deeper into the unknown, the crew must confront not only the secrets of the artifact but also the challenges of interstellar travel, political intrigue, and the limits of human knowledge."}]}
{"id": 155, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book La Belle Sauvage. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) La Belle Sauvage is a fantasy novel by Philip Pullman, serving as a prequel to His Dark Materials, following a young boy named Lyra Belacqua and her daemon Pantalaimon on a dangerous journey to protect an infant named Malcolm against malevolent forces. \n(B) The 5th Wave is a science fiction novel that follows a teenage girl's struggle to survive and reunite with her family after an alien invasion has decimated humanity and taken on various forms to eliminate the remaining survivors.\n(C) The 5th Wave is a young adult science fiction novel by Rick Yancey, set in a post-apocalyptic world where Earth is under attack by an alien species known as the Others. The story follows 16-year-old Cassie Sullivan as she navigates a series of four waves of increasingly devastating invasions that decimate humanity. In the aftermath of the fourth wave, Cassie must not only survive but also find her younger brother, Sam, who has been taken by the Others. Along the way, she encounters other survivors, forms alliances, and uncovers the truth about the aliens' ultimate plan, leading to a race against time to save herself, her brother, and possibly the remnants of humanity. The novel combines elements of action, suspense, and romance, as Cassie learns to trust and fight back against an enemy that seems to understand and exploit human nature better than humans themselves.\n(D) La Belle Sauvage is a prequel to Philip Pullman's popular \"His Dark Materials\" trilogy, set in a parallel world where humans possess animal companions called daemons and a powerful church-like organization influences society. The story follows a young boy named Lyra Belacqua and her friend Roger as they become embroiled in a dangerous adventure when they discover a mysterious girl named Pan, who is being pursued by dangerous forces. Together, they navigate the rivers of Oxfordshire on a perilous journey to protect the girl and uncover the truth behind a sinister plot that threatens the balance of their world."}]}
{"id": 156, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Witching Hour. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Manhattan Transfer is a popular jazz and pop vocal group known for their intricate harmonies and genre-blending approach, combining elements of swing, doo-wop, and contemporary styles. \n(B) The Witching Hour is a gothic novel by Anne Rice, published in 1990. It is the first installment in Rice's Mayfair Witches series and revolves around the lives of three powerful women: Rowan Mayfair, her daughter, and her grandmother, who are interconnected through their ancestral ties to a long line of witches. The story delves into the dark history of the Mayfair family, their magical abilities, and the struggle between good and evil as they navigate love, betrayal, and the forces that seek to destroy them. Set against the backdrop of New Orleans, the novel explores themes of family legacy, destiny, and the fine line between sanity and madness.\n(C) The Witching Hour is a novel by Anne Rice that tells the story of the lives, loves, and supernatural experiences of the Mayfair family, a dynasty of witches in New Orleans, spanning multiple generations.\n(D) Manhattan Transfer is a novel by American author Upton Sinclair, published in 1925. Set in the early 20th century, it chronicles the lives of various immigrants from different backgrounds as they navigate the challenges and hardships of life in New York City. The novel portrays the gritty reality of urban life, depicting the immigrants' struggles with poverty, exploitation, and the pursuit of the American Dream. Through its interconnected stories, Manhattan Transfer exposes the social and economic inequalities of the time, while also highlighting the resilience and diversity of the human spirit. Sinclair's work combines elements of naturalism and social commentary, making it a significant literary contribution to the portrayal of urban life and the immigrant experience in America."}]}
{"id": 157, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Predictably Irrational. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Palm-of-the-Hand Stories is a collection of微型 fiction pieces by Japanese author Banana Yoshimoto, exploring themes of love, loss, and memory through delicate and poetic narratives. \n(B) Palm-of-the-Hand Stories is a collection of微型 fiction by Japanese author Banana Yoshimoto. Consisting of 24 brief tales, each no longer than a page, the stories explore themes of love, loss, memory, and the ephemeral nature of life. With简约 and poetic prose, Yoshimoto captures moments of emotional depth and connection, often delving into the complexities of human relationships and the subtle ways in which the past influences the present. These concise narratives invite readers to reflect on the fleeting beauty and sadness that permeate our everyday lives.\n(C) Predictably Irrational, written by Dan Ariely, is a book that challenges conventional wisdom by exploring the hidden forces that influence our decision-making processes. Ariely, a behavioral economist, uses experiments and real-life examples to demonstrate how we often deviate from rational behavior, driven by biases, emotions, and social influences. He reveals how we consistently make predictable yet seemingly irrational choices in areas such as pricing, happiness, and social norms, demonstrating that our behavior is not always guided by logical reasoning. The book aims to open our eyes to the irrational patterns in our decision-making and encourages us to question our assumptions about rationality in everyday life.\n(D) Predictably Irrational is a book by Dan Ariely that explores the various ways in which human behavior defies traditional economic theory, revealing the irrational patterns and biases that influence our decision-making."}]}
{"id": 158, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Fabric of the Cosmos. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Joseph and His Brothers is a tetralogy of novels by Thomas Mann, exploring the biblical story of Joseph and his family, delving into themes of power, jealousy, and religious and sexual identity. \n(B) \"The Fabric of the Cosmos\" is a book by renowned physicist Brian Greene, which explores the fundamental nature of reality and the underlying principles that govern our universe. Through engaging storytelling and accessible explanations, Greene delves into concepts such as space, time, matter, and the unifying theories of physics, including string theory. He invites readers on a journey through the mind-bending ideas of modern physics, discussing the mysteries of black holes, the multiverse hypothesis, and the quest to find a Theory of Everything. Greene aims to deepen our understanding of the cosmos and our place within it, encouraging readers to ponder the profound questions at the frontier of scientific knowledge.\n(C) \"The Fabric of the Cosmos\" is a book by Brian Greene that explores the fundamental nature of reality, delving into concepts such as space, time, and the underlying principles that govern the universe.\n(D) Joseph and His Brothers is a tetralogy of novels by German author Thomas Mann, spanning over 1,000 pages in total. The epic work retells the biblical story of Joseph, a favorite son of Jacob, who is sold into slavery by his jealous brothers. The narrative follows Joseph's journey from Canaan to Egypt, where he rises to prominence as a prophetic advisor to the Pharaoh. It explores themes of family, betrayal, forgiveness, and the complex relationship between power, faith, and personal identity. Mann's version weaves historical, psychological, and mythic elements, transforming the biblical account into a profound exploration of human nature and the human condition."}]}
{"id": 159, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Seven Brief Lessons on Physics. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) This Side of Paradise is a novel by F. Scott Fitzgerald that follows the romantic experiences and societal disillusionment of young adulthood, centering on the lives of Amory Blaine, his wife Rosalind, and his friend Sally Jordan. \n(B) \"Seven Brief Lessons on Physics\" by Carlo Rovelli is a concise and accessible overview of the fundamental principles and theories in modern physics. Through seven short chapters, Rovelli elucidates complex concepts such as quantum mechanics, relativity, and the mysteries of the universe, including black holes and the origins of time. Aimed at a general audience, the book provides a thought-provoking introduction to the latest scientific understanding of our world, encouraging readers to ponder the profound implications of these theories on our perception of reality.\n(C) This Side of Paradise is F. Scott Fitzgerald's debut novel, published in 1920. It tells the story of Amory Blaine, a young, handsome, and intelligent Princeton University student who is disillusioned with traditional values and society's expectations. Through his romantic pursuits of two women, the sophisticated Rosalind Hunter and the innocent Sally Tomlinson, Amory navigates the complexities of love, class, and the post-World War I era. The novel explores themes of idealism, the decline of the American aristocracy, and the harsh realities that confront the young as they come of age. It also serves as an autobiographical reflection of Fitzgerald's own experiences, capturing the spirit of the Jazz Age and setting the stage for his later masterpiece, The Great Gatsby.\n(D) \"Seven Brief Lessons on Physics\" by Carlo Rovelli is a concise and accessible overview of key concepts in modern physics, ranging from quantum mechanics to black holes, written for a general audience."}]}
{"id": 160, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Under a White Sky. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Help\" is a novel by Kathryn Stockett that tells the story of African American maids working in white households in Jackson, Mississippi during the 1960s, and their relationships with the white women they work for. \n(B) Under a White Sky, written by Elizabeth Kolbert, is a thought-provoking exploration of humanity's attempts to reverse the impacts of climate change through geoengineering, delving into the ethical, environmental, and unintended consequences of these interventions. Kolbert weaves together personal anecdotes, scientific research, and encounters with scientists and visionaries to examine the limits of human intervention, ultimately questioning whether these desperate measures can truly save the planet or if they merely obscure our responsibility to address the root causes of climate change. The book serves as a cautionary tale, highlighting the complexity of nature and the need for a more sustainable relationship with our environment.\n(C) Under a White Sky by Elizabeth Kolbert explores the impact of human attempts to manipulate the environment, from climate change to geoengineering, and the complex ethical and ecological consequences that arise.\n(D) The Help is a novel by Kathryn Stockett, set in Jackson, Mississippi, during the early 1960s, which tells the story of African American maids working for white families and their experiences with racial inequality and discrimination. The narrative is narrated by three main characters: the young white woman Eugenia \"Skeeter\" Phelan, who aspires to be a writer and decides to write a book from the maids' perspectives, Aibileen Clark, a devoted maid and mother, and Minny Jackson, a bold and sassy maid. Through their stories, the book explores themes of friendship, courage, and the power of truth, as the maids risk their jobs and safety to share their stories, ultimately sparking change in their community."}]}
{"id": 161, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book From Bacteria to Bach and Back. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) From Bacteria to Bach and Back is a book by Douglas Hofstadter that explores the connections between complex systems, creativity, and the evolution of human thought through a series of interconnected essays and puzzles. \n(B) From Bacteria to Bach and Back: The Evolution of Music is a book by cognitive scientist and musician Daniel Levitin that explores the origins and evolution of music in the context of human and biological development. Levitin delves into various aspects of music, including its neurological impact, the evolutionary advantages it may have provided, and its connections to other cognitive processes such as language, emotion, and spatial reasoning. The book also discusses the universality of music across cultures, the nature of musical talent, and the role of music in society. By examining the intricate relationship between biology, psychology, and culture, Levitin aims to provide insights into why humans create and enjoy music, and how it has shaped our species throughout history.\n(C) \"The Black Dahlia\" is a crime drama film that follows two detectives investigating the gruesome murder of aspiring actress Elizabeth Short, known as the \"Black Dahlia,\" in 1947 Los Angeles.\n(D) \"The Black Dahlia\" is a crime novel by James Ellroy, published in 1947, that revolves around the investigation of the gruesome murder of Elizabeth Short, a young woman whose mutilated body was found in Los Angeles in 1947. The story follows two detectives, Bucky Bleichert and Lee Blanchard, as they delve into the dark underbelly of Hollywood, uncovering a web of corruption, obsession, and betrayal. The novel explores themes of violence, sexual deviance, and the quest for truth in a city where dreams and nightmares collide."}]}
{"id": 162, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book American Prometheus. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Jade War is a fantasy novel that continues the story of the Green Bone Saga, following the conflict between rival clans in a world where powerful magical jade is a source of great wealth and danger. \n(B) \"American Prometheus: The Triumph and Tragedy of J. Robert Oppenheimer\" is a biography written by Kai Bird and Martin Sherwin that chronicles the life of J. Robert Oppenheimer, the renowned physicist who played a central role in the development of the atomic bomb during World War II. The book delves into Oppenheimer's complex personality, his contributions to science, and the ethical implications of his work. It also explores his subsequent fall from grace due to his perceived Communist ties and the controversial security hearing that ultimately led to the loss of his security clearance. \"American Prometheus\" offers a comprehensive and nuanced portrait of a man whose genius and personal struggles shaped the course of history.\n(C) \"American Prometheus: The Triumph and Tragedy of J. Robert Oppenheimer\" by Kai Bird and Martin Sherwin is a biography that chronicles the life of the physicist who led the Manhattan Project, exploring his contributions to the development of the atomic bomb and his later controversial role as a public figure.\n(D) Jade War is the second installment in Fonda Lee's Green Bone Saga, a martial arts fantasy series. It continues the story of the Kaul family, who wield magical jade powers and control the criminal underworld of the city of Lion城. As the clan war between the Kauls and their rivals, the Aunts, escalates, the stakes become higher and involve political intrigue, international diplomacy, and the discovery of powerful new abilities. The novel explores themes of family, loyalty, and the corrupting influence of power, while delving deeper into the richly imagined world of Liang and its complex societal structures."}]}
{"id": 163, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The March of Folly. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) August 1914 marked the beginning of World War I, a global conflict that lasted until 1918. Following the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria by a Serbian nationalist, a complex web of alliances and tensions between European powers led to a rapid escalation of hostilities. Germany, seeking to assert its dominance and pursue its foreign policy objectives, invaded Belgium and declared war on France, while Russia mobilized to defend its ally. This triggered a chain reaction, with Austria-Hungary, Germany's ally, declaring war on Russia, and then on Serbia. Britain, bound by its treaty obligations, entered the conflict on the side of France and Russia, leading to the formation of two opposing alliances: the Allies (France, Russia, Britain, and later Italy, the United States, and others) and the Central Powers (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Ottoman Empire, and Bulgaria). The events of August 1914 set the stage for four years of brutal warfare, with devastating consequences for millions of lives and the geopolitical landscape of Europe. \n(B) The March of Folly: From Troy to Vietnam, written by Barbara Tuchman, is a historical analysis that examines instances of government folly across different civilizations and time periods. Tuchman argues that despite access to knowledge and opportunities for wise decision-making, leaders repeatedly make irrational and self-destructive choices. She explores cases like the Peloponnesian War, the Crusades, the Spanish Armada, the American Revolution, and the Vietnam War, highlighting the factors that contribute to such decisions, including bureaucratic inertia, political pressures, and ideological blinders. Tuchman's work serves as a cautionary tale, emphasizing the importance of learning from historical mistakes to avoid repeating them in the future.\n(C) August 1914 marked the beginning of World War I, as countries formed alliances and declared war, leading to a global conflict that lasted until 1918.\n(D) The March of Folly by Barbara Tuchman examines historical instances of government folly, arguing that盲目自信、偏见和官僚主义常常导致明知故犯的错误。"}]}
{"id": 164, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Other Minds. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Other Minds, by Peter Godfrey-Smith, explores the evolution of cognition and consciousness in animals, challenging our assumptions about the limits of intelligent and mindful beings in the natural world. \n(B) The Seven Husbands of Evelyn Hugo is a novel about a legendary Hollywood actress named Evelyn Hugo, who tells the story of her life and her seven marriages to various men over the course of her eventful career.\n(C) Other Minds, written by cognitive scientist and philosopher Daniel Dennett, explores the concept of consciousness and its relationship with the external world. Dennett argues that consciousness is not a mysterious, private phenomenon, but rather an emergent property of complex systems. He challenges traditional views of mind-body dualism and proposes an alternative, naturalistic account of subjective experience, emphasizing the importance of embodied cognition and the interaction between minds and their environments. Dennett draws on insights from various disciplines, including neuroscience, artificial intelligence, and evolutionary theory, to support his thesis that the appearance of subjective experience can be explained without appealing to supernatural or non-physical explanations.\n(D) \"The Seven Husbands of Evelyn Hugo\" is a novel by Taylor Jenkins Reid that follows the life of the glamorous and enigmatic Hollywood actress Evelyn Hugo, as she recounts her incredible journey and scandalous love affairs to an unknown magazine reporter, Monique Grant. Through a series of flashbacks, Evelyn reveals the secrets behind her seven marriages, her rise to fame, and the sacrifices she made in her personal life for the sake of her career. The story delves into themes of identity, fame, love, and the price of staying true to oneself in a world that constantly tries to define you."}]}
{"id": 165, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Quiet: The Power of Introverts. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The House on the Hill\" is a board game where players take on the role of investigators exploring a mysterious, haunted mansion. Each game generates a unique layout through a modular board design, adding to the replayability. Players navigate through rooms,遭遇恐怖事件, 解决谜题, and face off against supernatural creatures. The ultimate goal is to discover the truth behind the house's dark past while trying to survive the haunt's dangers. However, victory is not guaranteed, as the game also includes multiple endings, adding to its suspense and replay value. \n(B) \"Quiet: The Power of Introverts\" by Susan Cain explores the often-overlooked strengths and contributions of introverted individuals in a society that values extroversion. Cain challenges the common assumption that being outgoing and assertive is essential for success, arguing that introverts, with their tendency towards reflection, empathy, and deep thinking, bring unique advantages to various aspects of life. Through research, personal anecdotes, and insights from experts, she dismantles the myth of the \"ideal\" extrovert, advocating for a more balanced understanding and appreciation of introversion, and encouraging introverts to embrace their nature and harness their quiet power.\n(C) \"Quiet: The Power of Introverts\" by Susan Cain explores the misunderstood qualities and strengths of introverted individuals in a society that often favors extroversion, advocating for the importance of both types in achieving success and well-being.\n(D) \"The House on the Hill\" is a board game where players explore a haunted mansion, facing恐怖 and making choices that determine their fate."}]}
{"id": 166, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Blue Ocean Strategy. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Emperor of All Maladies: A Biography of Cancer\" by Siddhartha Mukherjee is a comprehensive and Pulitzer Prize-winning account of cancer, tracing its history from ancient times to the cutting-edge of modern science. The book delves into the personal stories of patients, doctors, and researchers, while exploring the various scientific breakthroughs, failures, and the emotional toll of the disease. It provides a nuanced understanding of cancer as a complex and relentless enemy, and chronicles the relentless pursuit of treatments and cures, offering hope and a glimpse into the future of oncology. \n(B) Blue Ocean Strategy is a management framework that emphasizes the creation of uncontested market space by identifying and pursuing new opportunities rather than competing in existing, crowded markets. Developed by W. Chan Kim and Renée Mauborgne, the approach encourages businesses to innovate and differentiate themselves by focusing on value innovation, which simultaneously creates value for customers and generates profits for the company. By looking beyond industry boundaries and redefining the boundaries of competition, firms can create \"blue oceans\" of uncontested market space, where there is little or no competition, and thus achieve sustainable growth and success.\n(C) Blue Ocean Strategy is a management framework that focuses on creating uncontested market space and finding new revenue opportunities by innovating and offering unique value propositions, rather than competing in crowded and price-driven markets.\n(D) The Emperor of All Maladies is a comprehensive history of cancer, from its ancient origins to the latest scientific advancements in cancer treatment, exploring the emotional and societal impact of the disease."}]}
{"id": 167, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Free to Choose. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"Free to Choose\" is a book and television series by economist Milton Friedman that advocates for free market capitalism and individual liberty as the best paths to economic prosperity and personal freedom. \n(B) \"Chronicle of a Blood Merchant\" is a novel by Chinese author Yu Hua that follows the life of a blood seller in rural China during the mid-20th century, exploring themes of poverty, survival, and the human condition.\n(C) \"Chronicle of a Blood Merchant\" is a novel by Chinese author Yu Hua, which tells the story of a young man named Chen Zhen who becomes a blood donor in a rural Chinese village during the 1960s. As China undergoes political动荡 and economic hardships, Chen exploits the demand for blood to make a living, eventually turning into a blood entrepreneur. The novel explores themes of survival, morality, and the human condition in the face of adversity, reflecting on the complexities of Chinese society and the consequences of prioritizing individual gain over collective well-being.\n(D) \"Free to Choose\" is a book and television series by economist Milton Friedman, in which he advocates for free market capitalism and individual liberty. Friedman argues that economic freedom is essential for personal freedom and prosperity, and he critiques government intervention in the economy, asserting that it often does more harm than good. He examines various policies, such as minimum wage laws, price controls, and centralized planning, demonstrating how they can distort markets and hinder economic growth. Friedman champions deregulation, privatization, and a simplified tax system as solutions to improve social welfare and foster a thriving society."}]}
{"id": 168, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book This Changes Everything. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Snow is a novel by Orson Scott Card that tells the story of a future Earth where global warming has caused the polar ice caps to melt, leading to a new ice age, and follows a young man named Snow as he navigates a post-apocalyptic world and uncovers secrets about his past. \n(B) \"This Changes Everything\" is a book written by Naomi Klein that presents a comprehensive analysis of the interconnectivity between capitalism and climate change. Klein argues that the current economic system, driven by relentless growth and corporate greed, is the primary driver of the global environmental crisis. She asserts that addressing climate change requires a fundamental transformation of our economic model, challenging the status quo and advocating for a just transition towards sustainable and equitable societies. The book not only provides a dire warning about the consequences of inaction but also offers a call to action, highlighting the potential for collective mobilization and systemic change to create a better future for all.\n(C) Snow is a novel by Orson Scott Card, set in a dystopian future where the Earth is covered in ice and humanity survives in massive, mobile cities that constantly move across the frozen surface to avoid the harsh conditions. The story follows a young man named Todd, who lives in one of these cities and discovers a dark secret about their society's past. As he tries to escape and expose the truth, Todd must navigate treacherous landscapes, confront authority figures, and discover his own strength and resilience. Snow explores themes of totalitarianism, individuality, and the power of knowledge, while blending elements of science fiction and adventure.\n(D) \"This Changes Everything\" by Naomi Klein is a book that argues that addressing climate change requires a fundamental rethinking of our economic and social systems, and presents a case for radical transformative change."}]}
{"id": 169, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Medium Raw. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) Alias Grace is a novel by Margaret Atwood that tells the story of Grace Marks, a young Irish immigrant convicted of murder in 1843 Canada, and the psychiatrist who becomes obsessed with uncovering the truth behind her crimes. \n(B) Medium Raw is a memoir by chef and television personality Anthony Bourdain, published in 2010. In the book, Bourdain offers a no-holds-barred look at the culinary world, providing a raw and unapologetic account of his experiences working in professional kitchens, from his early days as a line cook to his rise to fame as a chef and TV host. He dishes on the cutthroat nature of the industry, shares anecdotes about famous chefs and fellow culinary personalities, and reflects on his own personal struggles, including drug addiction, infidelity, and the challenges of balancing career and family. The book is known for its gritty, unfiltered writing style and offers a behind-the-scenes look at the high-pressure and often chaotic world of fine dining.\n(C) Alias Grace is a novel by Margaret Atwood that tells the story of Grace Marks, a young Irish immigrant in 19th-century Canada who was convicted of murder. The narrative alternates between Grace's own account of her life and the efforts of a doctor named Simon Jordan to uncover the truth about the crimes through a series of interviews with her. As Grace's story unfolds, it delves into themes of memory, identity, women's roles, and the nature of guilt and innocence. Atwood weaves a complex and haunting tale, blurring the lines between fact and fiction, while exploring the societal pressures and personal demons that may have driven Grace to commit the heinous acts for which she was accused.\n(D) Medium Raw is a memoir by Anthony Bourdain in which he shares his experiences and insights from the culinary world, offering a raw and unapologetic look at the restaurant industry."}]}
{"id": 170, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Being and Nothingness. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The BFG is a heartwarming story about a young orphan girl who befriends a kind-hearted giant and embarks on a magical adventure to stop the man-eating giants that threaten their world. \n(B) \"Being and Nothingness\" by Jean-Paul Sartre explores the nature of human existence, positing that individuals are fundamentally conscious beings who create their own meaning through自由意志 and constant choice in the face of an inherently meaningless universe.\n(C) The BFG (Big Friendly Giant) is a heartwarming story by Roald Dahl, following the adventures of a young orphan girl named Sophie. One night, Sophie is whisked away by the BFG from London to his home in Giant Country. She discovers that he is different from the other giants who eat humans, as he captures dreams and protects children. Together, they form an unlikely friendship and devise a plan to stop the man-eating giants, with the help of the Queen of England. Through courage, teamwork, and the power of dreams, they save the day and ensure a happier future for all.\n(D) \"Being and Nothingness\" by Jean-Paul Sartre is a comprehensive exploration of human existence and consciousness. Sartre argues that human beings are fundamentally \"radically free\" and that our essence is defined by our constant choices and actions, rather than being predetermined by nature or a higher power. He asserts that we create our own meaning in a world that is essentially meaningless, and that our very consciousness of being brings about the experience of \"nothingness\" or the absence of inherent purpose. The book delves into various philosophical concepts such as anxiety, authenticity, and the Other, as Sartre seeks to understand the human condition through a rigorous examination of the relationship between being and consciousness."}]}
{"id": 171, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Power of Myth. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) The Power of Myth is a book by Joseph Campbell that explores the enduring significance of myths in human culture, examining how these stories reflect our shared archetypal experiences and offer profound wisdom for navigating life's journey. \n(B) Farewell, My Lovely is a hard-boiled detective novel by Raymond Chandler, featuring private investigator Philip Marlowe, who gets entangled in a complex web of deception, murder, and romance while searching for a missing woman.\n(C) Farewell, My Lovely is a hard-boiled detective novel by Raymond Chandler, featuring his iconic character Philip Marlowe. The story follows Marlowe as he becomes entangled in a complex web of deception and danger when he is hired by a wealthy businessman to find his missing ex-girlfriend, Vivian Sternwood Rutledge. As Marlowe delves deeper into the case, he encounters a cast of suspicious characters, including Vivian's sister, Carmen, and a ruthless gangster named Moose Malloy. Amidst the intrigue, Marlowe navigates the dark underbelly of Los Angeles, facing perilous encounters and unraveling a conspiracy that threatens his life. Chandler's vivid prose and vivid depiction of the 1940s detective genre make Farewell, My Lovely a classic of crime fiction.\n(D) \"The Power of Myth\" is a book by Joseph Campbell, a renowned mythologist, in collaboration with Bill Moyers. The book is a collection of conversations that explore the enduring role of mythology in human life. It delves into various myths and stories from different cultures, examining how they reflect our shared human experiences, archetypes, and the search for meaning. Campbell emphasizes the importance of understanding myths as symbolic narratives that can provide guidance, inspire personal growth, and connect us to a deeper spiritual dimension, transcending religious and cultural boundaries. The book invites readers to see their own lives as part of a larger mythic journey, encouraging them to discover and embrace their own hero's path."}]}
{"id": 172, "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book Sapiens: A Graphic History. Please respond with only \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\".\n(A) \"The Captain's Daughter\" is a novel by Russian author Alexander Pushkin, first published in 1836. The story is set in St. Petersburg and Crimea during the Pugachev Rebellion of 1773-1775. It revolves around the life of Marya, the beautiful daughter of a Russian army captain, who falls in love with Grigory Alexandrovich Pechorin, a charismatic and enigmatic officer. As Marya navigates the complexities of love, loyalty, and societal expectations, she becomes entangled in a web of intrigue, betrayal, and ultimately, tragedy. Through Marya's eyes, Pushkin explores themes of passion, honor, and the harsh realities of war, offering a vivid portrayal of 18th-century Russian society. \n(B) Sapiens: A Graphic History is a visually captivating adaptation of Yuval Noah Harari's best-selling book, Sapiens: A Brief History of Humankind. This graphic novel presents a condensed, illustrated journey through the history of our species, from the emergence of Homo sapiens in Africa to the present-day globalized world. It explores key milestones such as the cognitive revolution, the agricultural revolution, the rise of empires, and the scientific revolution, all while examining the impact of these developments on human societies, beliefs, and relationships with other species. The graphic novel format combines compelling artwork with concise narrative to make complex ideas accessible and engaging for a wider audience.\n(C) Sapiens: A Graphic History is a visually illustrated adaptation of Yuval Noah Harari's best-selling book, presenting a concise overview of human history from the emergence of our species to the present day.\n(D) \"The Captain's Daughter\" by Richard Brinsley Sheridan is a play that revolves around the romantic entanglements and adventures of the daughter of a navy captain during the Age of Sail."}]}
